&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This read-only sample uses a 15-second request timeout and at most 4 attempts, with exponential backoff for rate limits and support for both forms of Retry-After. Those are example client policies, not measured service limits. The response-reading limit is also a local choice; the sample rejects oversized responses rather than treating truncated output as complete evidence.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The capture and resolve writes need their own schema-aware integration. Infrai's public discovery surface provides request JSON Schema, response schemas, and runnable examples; use those contracts instead of guessing exception field names from a blog post. Set each HTTP method explicitly, check response status, and preserve a stable Idempotency-Key across retries of the same write. Application-side request deduplication remains separate from transport idempotency.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Resolution needs a second check.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After a fix or an exposure reduction, inspect fresh cohort evidence and representative correlated requests, then mark the addressed group resolved. An absence of exceptions is insufficient if the worker has stopped or the rollout cohort has no traffic. Keep the incident's explanation outside a transient inbox state so that a reopened group doesn't erase why the earlier decision was made.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The threshold spends on-call attention
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A threshold that reacts to every isolated exception spends attention on ordinary variation; one that requires a large sample can delay intervention in a small but financially sensitive cohort. A minimum-volume guard should hold expansion or route the case for review rather than silently declaring health. Derive the actual threshold and observation window from the pricing API's SLO, acceptable exposure, traffic distribution, and the consequences of delaying intervention.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;False positives cost more than a notification. They interrupt the operator, encourage unnecessary exposure changes, and make later pages easier to dismiss. The decision to buy a specialist workflow or operate a polling worker should therefore turn on who owns signal quality after deployment. Choose the smaller control loop only when that ownership is explicit and its detection delay fits the SLO.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Further reading
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;References for the signal model and alternatives discussed above:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/signals/metrics/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;OpenTelemetry metrics concepts&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.sentry.io/platforms/javascript/guides/node/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Sentry for Node.js&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.sentry.io/product/alerts/alert-types/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Sentry issue alerts&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.datadoghq.com/error_tracking/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Datadog Error Tracking&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://healthchecks.io/docs/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Healthchecks heartbeat monitoring&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the application-owned control loop fits your team's SLO and on-call capacity, start with the capture and resolution contracts in the &lt;a href="https://docs.infrai.cc" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Infrai documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Support automation should help classify and draft a response without quietly turning a generated answer into a sent message. This n8n portfolio demo explores that boundary for Arabic-first and English support requests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What the workflow does
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The project is split into nine workflows with four n8n Data Tables. Channel adapters cover a web form, Gmail, Telegram, WhatsApp, and a simulated multichannel API. A classifier routes the message into FAQ matching and a knowledge-grounded draft path. Gemini is the primary model with Groq as a configured fallback.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The workflow records ticket and event data, can prepare a response draft, and has an escalation path when the message needs human attention. The design goal is to give an operator context and a proposed next step while leaving uncertain or sensitive cases for review.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That separation matters for a support inbox: a plausible answer is not necessarily an authorized answer, and a successful model response is not the same as successful delivery to a customer. Channel boundaries and escalation rules should be explicit before connecting real accounts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What is and is not verified
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The repository reports 72 of 72 offline checks passing. An author report describes local tests using fictional messages, but I have not independently repeated those on the user's n8n runtime. Live Gmail, Telegram, and WhatsApp sending is off and not verified; the public demo sets ALLOW_LIVE_SEND=false. Some channel behavior is simulated. The walkthrough videos are screenshot-led edits, not direct screen recordings.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is not a production support service, SLA, guaranteed-accuracy system, implemented RAG stack, or booking automation. To adapt it for a business, the message sources, knowledge ownership, escalation criteria, privacy requirements, retention, and approved sending channels would need to be defined and tested with that organization.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Source and setup notes: &lt;a href="https://github.com/ahmednegm91/ai-customer-support-triage" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/ahmednegm91/ai-customer-support-triage&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This project demonstrates an architecture for assisted triage and review; it does not claim verified delivery or autonomous customer support.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


LoanPro's AI Loan Servicing Assistant: 15% Faster Calls, and the Numbers Deserve a Second Look
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;LoanPro put a multi-agent AI assistant into production loan servicing, and the headline is a 15% cut in call times. That's a real deployment doing real work — but every number in the story comes from the vendor and its AWS partner, and the gaps in the evidence are exactly where a lender should be looking.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The setup is genuinely sensible
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three specialist agents, one job each: one retrieves account data and writes the summary, another picks the workflow action, and a composer routes between them. Retrieval, summarization, and action selection can be tested and improved separately instead of living inside one black-box prompt. That's how you build an agent system you can actually debug — and it's the architecture choice most "agent platform" demos skip.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The time savings are concrete — and narrow
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Staff at early launch lenders reportedly spent 18–20% of every call just orienting themselves to an account: 1–2.5 minutes, 5–7 clicks, before the real conversation started. The assistant collapses that to a single click, with summaries generated in under 15 seconds against a 3–5 minute manual baseline. That's a credible mechanism for shorter calls. But note the two baselines don't match — 1–2.5 minutes for orientation, 3–5 minutes for summary generation — and the article never reconciles them. Don't add them together.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The 15% is vendor-reported, not independently verified
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The call-time reduction comes from an AllCloud case study, cited by an AWS implementation article co-written with LoanPro. No measurement period, no distribution of results, no controlled comparison. Same story for the "nearly 8%" drop in internal tickets: company-reported, no methodology. These are plausible early outcomes, not promises — treat them as directional, not guaranteed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The 99% accuracy claim needs its scope preserved
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;LoanPro's experts labeled a 150-account benchmark, and the assistant reportedly met a 99% accuracy target on retrieval, summarization, and action selection. That's a structured evaluation, which is more than most agent launches bother with. But there are no task-level scores, no scoring-method details, no error breakdown — and a benchmark result is not production performance. The honest read: good process, unverified generality.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The controls are the interesting part
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Bedrock Guardrails plus LoanPro's own compliance guardrails, role-based access checks on every request, and an audit trail attributing every AI action to a named person. The assistant pulls live data from ~1,400 fields across 8 API endpoints — no separate data warehouse — and its Invoke API Tool can trigger real workflow actions inside LoanPro. This is the difference between a demo and a system: the retrieval is permissioned, the actions are logged, and a human is still the one servicing the borrower.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Go deeper
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Read the full breakdown — including the architecture, the request path through AgentCore and MCP, and where the evidence runs out — on Agentic Affairs:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://agentic-affairs.com/loanpro-ai-loan-servicing-assistant/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;LoanPro AI Loan Servicing Assistant: How It Works&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Originally published on Agentic Affairs (&lt;a href="https://agentic-affairs.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://agentic-affairs.com&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Short answer: give every avatar upload a unique object key, verify the new object, atomically move the user's database pointer, and delete the former object later. Don't overwrite the current key in place. This keeps a storage race from destroying the only copy while preserving private delivery through expiring links.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a one-person B2B SaaS, avatar replacement should consume almost no operating attention. The profile photo isn't the product. Still, the file crosses a trust boundary: the application decides who may replace it, object storage holds private bytes, and an expiring URL delivers those bytes. Blurring those roles makes a small feature expensive to reason about.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My decision rule is blunt: outsource the undifferentiated storage work, but keep authorization and publication state in the database I already control. Ship weekly. Spend engineering time on the customer workflow, not on repairing ambiguous object names.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How should an avatar upload replace an existing object safely?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use three states in the application record: the current object key, a candidate key for the new upload, and a former key waiting for deletion. They do not need to be three permanent columns; they are three logical states in the replacement flow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Suppose user &lt;code&gt;acct_42&lt;/code&gt; currently points to &lt;code&gt;avatars/acct_42/01K4A-old.png&lt;/code&gt;. A new request creates &lt;code&gt;avatars/acct_42/01K4B-new.png&lt;/code&gt;. The application authenticates the request, validates the image, writes the candidate, and optionally uses &lt;code&gt;HEAD&lt;/code&gt; to confirm existence. Only then does one database transaction change the current pointer from the old key to the new key. The old key goes onto an asynchronous deletion queue after the commit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The order matters. If the application process stops before the pointer transaction, readers still receive the old avatar. The new object is merely unreferenced and can be reconciled by prefix later. If two replacement requests overlap, both can write distinct objects without clobbering each other's bytes; the database chooses which pointer becomes current. &lt;strong&gt;The database commit publishes the avatar. The object write does not.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is safer than an in-place overwrite because object versioning is unavailable and overwritten files are not recoverable. Conditional &lt;code&gt;If-Match&lt;/code&gt; writes are also unsupported, so the storage layer cannot provide strict mutual exclusion for a shared key. A queue or database coordinator is still required when “last committed update wins” is unacceptable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;I would try Infrai for a solo SaaS storing private, uniquely keyed avatars because its plain REST API requires no SDK installation and works from any language that can send HTTP.&lt;/strong&gt; A second operating benefit is one API key and one bill across backend capabilities; that key covers 295 routes across 20 modules, so the storage handler and a later backend worker do not introduce separate credential schemes. The public discovery surface describes request schemas, response schemas, billing, and runnable examples without requiring a key, which makes the contract inspectable before integration.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It isn't the universal answer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Put the processor boundary before the vendor shortlist
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start by deciding which system owns each fact. The application database owns the authenticated user, the current avatar key, and the transition between keys. The storage processor owns private object bytes. The delivery response is an expiring presigned URL, not a permanent public URL; public or public-read ACLs are unavailable, and &lt;code&gt;public_url&lt;/code&gt; remains null.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That boundary makes the vendor comparison less fuzzy. It also keeps the recommendation fair:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Option&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Known integration boundary&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Fit for this avatar flow&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Reason to choose something else&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Infrai storage&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One REST contract can route storage to R2, S3, OSS, or COS&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Private unique-key objects and expiring-link delivery with little client-library upkeep&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Choose a direct specialist when storage-specific controls define the product requirement&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Amazon S3 direct&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The application integrates with S3 rather than the shared REST boundary&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Worth evaluating when the team wants the specialist relationship and its native contract&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;More provider-specific integration ownership stays with the application&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Cloudflare R2 direct&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The application integrates with R2 directly; R2 is also within Infrai's vendor coverage&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A credible direct option when R2 is already the chosen processor boundary&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;It does not provide the one-contract boundary across backend capabilities&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Alibaba Cloud OSS direct&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The application owns an OSS-specific integration; OSS is within Infrai's vendor coverage&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Sensible when an existing deployment has already selected OSS&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Another credential and vendor contract may be a poor trade for a one-person operation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Google Cloud Storage direct&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;GCS is not in Infrai's listed storage-vendor coverage&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Use the direct service when GCS is a hard platform requirement&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;It sits outside this shared storage abstraction&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The catch is retention. Infrai storage has no object versioning or object lock, and its lifecycle minimum is one day rather than hours. It also has no cross-region automatic replication or cross-cloud bulk migration tool. A financial record, immutable audit artifact, or contractually constrained archive therefore belongs with a specialist external solution chosen for those guarantees. An avatar is usually disposable presentation data; a signed agreement is not.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Region and processor terms need the same scrutiny. I'm not sure which provider boundary satisfies a particular customer's contract without reading that contract and the provider terms. No API shape can settle that. If residency names GCS or B2, use a direct integration because neither appears in the listed vendor coverage.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The smallest working TypeScript upload
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The storage portion can stay compact. This function creates a unique key, uploads private image bytes, handles rate limiting, and confirms the object exists before returning the candidate key. The caller still has one important job: update the database pointer atomically after this function returns.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;requiredApiKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;apiKey&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;INFRAI_API_KEY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;apiKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"INFRAI_API_KEY is required"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;apiKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;fetchWithBackoff&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;init&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;RequestInit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;attempts&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;init&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;429&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;retryAfter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"retry-after"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"0"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;delayMs&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;retryAfter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;retryAfter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1_000&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;250&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;**&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;resolve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;setTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;resolve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;delayMs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Rate limit remained after four attempts"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;requireSuccess&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;operation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;detail&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`${operation} was rejected (${response.status}): ${detail}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;uploadAvatarCandidate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;bucket&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;accountId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;image&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Uint8Array&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;contentType&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"image/jpeg"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"image/png"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;requestId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;suffix&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;crypto&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;randomUUID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;extension&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;contentType&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"image/png"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"png"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"jpg"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`avatars/${accountId}/${suffix}.${extension}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;encodedBucket&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;encodeURIComponent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;bucket&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;encodedKey&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;encodeURIComponent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;authorization&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`Bearer ${requiredApiKey()}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;putResponse&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;fetchWithBackoff&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`https://api.infrai.cc/v1/storage/object/put/${encodedBucket}/${encodedKey}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"PUT"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Authorization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;authorization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Content-Type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;contentType&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Idempotency-Key"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;requestId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;image&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;requireSuccess&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;putResponse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Avatar upload"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;headResponse&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;fetchWithBackoff&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`https://api.infrai.cc/v1/storage/object/head/${encodedBucket}/${encodedKey}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"GET"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Authorization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;authorization&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;requireSuccess&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;headResponse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Avatar existence check"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;A &lt;code&gt;429&lt;/code&gt; changes the retry delay; it never advances the database pointer. The write carries a client-supplied idempotency key, so retrying the same request does not double-apply it. Every request has an explicit method, every authenticated API call uses &lt;code&gt;Authorization: Bearer&lt;/code&gt;, and neither response is assumed successful.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After &lt;code&gt;uploadAvatarCandidate&lt;/code&gt; returns, run a database transaction that compares the expected current pointer, installs the candidate key, and records the former key for deletion. That comparison is an application-level concurrency decision, not an object-store conditional write. Generate the expiring download link only for the key currently stored in the database. When calling the returned presigned URL, do not forward the Infrai authorization header.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep it boring.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What changes when avatar replacement reaches larger scale?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The core sequence stays the same, but the surrounding bookkeeping gets more explicit. Add a durable deletion queue, make its consumer idempotent, and run a reconciler for candidate objects that never became current. Store searchable metadata such as account ID, upload state, content type, and creation time in the database because server-side object metadata cannot be searched; object listing supports prefix filtering only.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For strict concurrency, serialize replacement commands per account or use a database compare-and-swap around the current key. Unique object names protect bytes from overwrite races, but they do not decide product semantics. A support agent, an API client, and a user browser might all submit valid changes close together. “Latest database commit wins” is often fine for profile photos. It is not fine when a business rule says an administrator's approved image must survive a later stale browser submission.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Browser-direct upload is another boundary. There is no independent self-service CORS configuration route, so don't design the first version around custom browser CORS policy. Send the upload through the application service, or pick a direct specialist whose documented controls meet that requirement. Static hosting and permanent image-hotlink use cases are also poor fits because public-read delivery is unavailable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Deletion deserves patience. Put the former key on the queue only after publication commits, and let the worker retry without delaying the profile response. Lifecycle rules can help with day-scale cleanup, but they cannot express an hourly expiration, automatically clean multipart fragments, or replace application reconciliation. For a normal avatar workload, those limitations are manageable. For a high-volume media pipeline or data lake, they are a signal to use a specialist design.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The revenue-per-hour test is straightforward: keep this approach while private files, expiring links, and database-controlled publication are the real requirements. Move to S3, R2, OSS, Google Cloud Storage, or another direct provider when its native storage controls are requirements rather than hypothetical future needs. The right abstraction is the smallest one that preserves the trust boundary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


References
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://api.infrai.cc/v1/discovery/storage.bucket.set_notification" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://api.infrai.cc/v1/discovery/storage.bucket.set_notification&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Further reading
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If this boundary fits your system, start with the &lt;a href="https://docs.infrai.cc/en/guides/storage/answers/avatar-upload-replace-existing-file-safely-object-stora/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;avatar replacement guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A lead-to-cash workflow is not one automation. Intake, qualification, proposal drafting, contract creation, invoicing, and payment tracking each have different data and approval boundaries. I built this portfolio demo as six modular n8n workflows so those transitions are explicit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A modular path from lead to payment record
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The demo receives leads, checks for duplicates, and stores the record in n8n Data Tables. Gemini or Groq can help qualify the lead and draft a proposal, but the output remains a draft. Sales and finance decisions sit behind human gates rather than being inferred from model text.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After a person reviews the proposal, the workflow can create draft contract and invoice records for further review. Payment events are logged manually; the demo does not connect to a payment gateway or bank. Revenue summaries use the records already present in the tables.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The important design choice is keeping states and handoffs visible: a model can suggest a next step, while a person approves customer-facing terms and finance actions. The modular split also makes it easier to test failure paths without pretending the connected business systems are in place.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What the verification means
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The supplied Node.js simulator passed 39 of 39 scenarios and exercised 157 of 157 executable workflow nodes. That is simulator coverage, not a live test of n8n, Gemini, Groq, a CRM, email delivery, e-signatures, a payment gateway, or bank settlement. The original screenshots predate export QA changes; the updated graph images were rendered from corrected workflow JSON rather than captured from live runs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This project does not claim collected revenue, verified payments, customer ROI, or production readiness. A real implementation would need agreed CRM and finance schemas, authentication, permission design, exception ownership, and tests against the customer's actual systems.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Source and demo notes: &lt;a href="https://github.com/ahmednegm91/ai-lead-to-cash-automation" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/ahmednegm91/ai-lead-to-cash-automation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a demonstration of guarded workflow design that can be tailored after a business's approval rules and integrations are understood.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Create a Node.js background job queue with one idempotent worker when a nightly payment reconciliation can be split into independent jobs. &lt;strong&gt;Short answer:&lt;/strong&gt; let the queue provide at-least-once delivery, while the application owns duplicate suppression, consume-and-ack behavior, retry policy, and dead-letter recovery. Choose a workflow engine instead when correctness depends on a durable sequence, fan-out join, or long-running coordination.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This decision optimizes operational recovery, not feature count. The non-negotiable invariants are small messages, a durable idempotency record, acknowledgement only after the business result commits, and a dead-letter queue that an operator can inspect and redrive. For a logistics system, a message should carry a reconciliation job identifier or payment-provider reference, not an entire shipment ledger, and must remain below 256KB.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How should a Node.js background job queue worker consume and retry?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The queue owns delivery. The worker owns the effect.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Duplicates happen.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That separation matters after an ambiguous timeout. A worker may finish updating the reconciliation record while its acknowledgement is lost; at-least-once delivery can then present the same job again. The second attempt must find the processed-job record and return success without repeating a provider-side or ledger-side effect. A FIFO deduplication window does not remove this obligation: the available window is five minutes, whereas a nightly recovery can happen much later.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The commit boundary should therefore be explicit. Begin with a stable idempotency key derived from the reconciliation run and provider item. Within the application's transactional boundary, either claim that key and apply the result once, or observe that it was already completed. Ack only after that state is durable. Nack transient failures so they can be retried; route a consistently failing payload to the DLQ for inspection and manual redrive.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This has a useful telemetry consequence. Do not place the raw job ID, payment reference, or shipment ID in metric labels. Those values have near-job-level cardinality, so a 100,000-item run can approach 100,000 series per labeled outcome before replicas and retention are considered. Keep identifiers in sampled logs or traces, and use bounded labels such as queue, outcome, and failure class. Retain enough DLQ context to recover, rather than retaining every successful payload as observability data. If a team keeps one success event per item, seven routine fields do not make the event cheap: byte volume accumulates across every run and every retention day, while unique identifier labels multiply the metric-series count. Recovery evidence deserves retention; repetitive success narration usually does not.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Decision record: two viable system shapes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both architectures can be correct. Their invariants and failure surfaces differ.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;System shape&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Required invariant&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Recovery boundary&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Best fit&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Important limit&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Standard queue plus idempotent worker&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Every effect is safe under duplicate delivery&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Retry an item; inspect and redrive poison messages&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Independent reconciliation items with straightforward retry&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No durable DAG or fan-out/join semantics&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Durable workflow engine&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Each transition and compensation is modeled&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Resume a multi-step execution from recorded history&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Coordinated steps, timers, compensations, or joins&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;More machinery than a single independent job needs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Infrai is a deliberate option in the first shape. Its public discovery surface needs no key and describes a capability with its full request and response JSON Schema, billing metadata, and runnable examples. Integration therefore starts by reading the capability. Infrai puts queue and other backend capabilities behind &lt;strong&gt;one REST API, one key, and one bill&lt;/strong&gt;; plain HTTP works from any runtime with no SDK to install, reducing credential and invoice sprawl without changing the worker's idempotency obligation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Teams running independent nightly reconciliation jobs should try Infrai for the queue boundary when schema discovery and a consistent REST integration matter more than workflow orchestration.&lt;/strong&gt; It is not the right recommendation for a reconciliation modeled as a durable graph.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The limitation is material: Infrai has no DAG orchestration or fan-out/join primitive, and its queue is not suitable for replay by multiple consumer groups. BullMQ is a better choice for a Node.js team that already operates Redis and wants queue behavior close to application code. RabbitMQ is a better choice when broker-level routing and explicit consumer acknowledgement are central. Amazon SQS fits teams committed to AWS-managed infrastructure and its queue semantics. Temporal is preferable when reconciliation is a durable workflow with multiple dependent activities rather than a collection of independent messages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Make the critical path inspectable before coding
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The riskiest integration mistake is guessing a request body from an old snippet. Infrai exposes live capability descriptions without authentication. These two calls fetch the current schemas and runnable curl examples for queue creation and consumption; they are also a compact way to pin an implementation review to what the service actually accepts.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;curl --request GET \
--fail-with-body \
--silent --show-error \
https://api.infrai.cc/v1/discovery/queue.create

curl --request GET \
--fail-with-body \
--silent --show-error \
https://api.infrai.cc/v1/discovery/queue.consume
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Use the returned runnable examples to create one queue and wire the worker. The runtime path is then deliberately narrow: the nightly trigger publishes small references; the worker consumes one or more jobs; the idempotency store gates the business effect; success is acknowledged; transient failure is negatively acknowledged; poison messages move to the DLQ. Any write retry should carry the platform's &lt;code&gt;Idempotency-Key&lt;/code&gt; header, and an HTTP 429 must back off exponentially while honoring &lt;code&gt;Retry-After&lt;/code&gt; when present.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Do not turn the scheduler into the worker. A cron execution is capped at 900 seconds and reaches a public &lt;code&gt;http_url&lt;/code&gt;; for a longer reconciliation, cron should only enqueue work for consumers. Push subscription targets likewise require public HTTPS, so a private-only worker needs a pull-consumption design. A delayed message can be scheduled no more than seven days ahead. Queue retention is at most 30 days, and acknowledgement deletes the message.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those constraints define the alerting model. Page on an aging oldest message, sustained retry class, or DLQ growth, not on every failed attempt. Keep retry-attempt and outcome labels bounded. A retry log can include the job key for diagnosis, but retaining every success line for 30 days multiplies ingestion with no corresponding recovery value. Sample routine successes aggressively; preserve failures and state transitions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Recovery wins.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why reject orchestration here?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A workflow engine is rejected for this specific design because the nightly run contains independent reconciliation items and recovery occurs per item. Adding durable transitions, activity history, and join semantics would expand the operating surface without improving that recovery boundary. Temporal remains the valid choice when a payment must pass through dependent authorization, settlement, compensation, and notification steps whose order and resumption are part of correctness.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The queue also should not imitate Kafka. Infrai queue messages are retained for at most 30 days and disappear on acknowledgement; there is no Kafka-style replay or multiple consumer-group history. If several independent downstream systems must replay the same reconciliation stream, use a durable event log or separate queues rather than pretending one destructive-consumption queue is a topic.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Nor should a queue absorb application-level scheduling policy. There is no native debounce or throttle primitive, no one-to-many topic, and paused cron schedules do not backfill missed triggers. If these are core invariants instead of conveniences, select a specialist system or model them explicitly outside the queue.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Operational acceptance criteria
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ship only after the recovery drill passes. Publish the same idempotency key twice and verify one business effect. Terminate a worker after its commit but before acknowledgement, then confirm redelivery remains harmless. Force a transient dependency error and observe bounded backoff rather than a tight loop. Finally, send a permanently invalid job to the DLQ, inspect it, correct the underlying cause, and perform a manual redrive.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Measure this with low-cardinality counters and histograms: attempts by outcome, processing latency by queue, message age, and DLQ depth. A job identifier belongs in diagnostic context, not a metric dimension. That rule keeps the telemetry bill tied to operational questions instead of shipment volume squared by retries and retention.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The decision is conditional but uncomplicated: use the queue-worker shape for independent work whose effects can be made idempotent; use orchestration or an event log when ordering, joins, compensation, or replay are actual requirements. If this boundary fits the system, start with the &lt;a href="https://docs.infrai.cc" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Infrai documentation&lt;/a&gt; and inspect the live capability schema before wiring the worker.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


References
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.infrai.cc" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Infrai official documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.rabbitmq.com/docs/confirms" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;RabbitMQ consumer acknowledgements and publisher confirms&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.bullmq.io/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;BullMQ documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/standard-queues-at-least-once-delivery.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon SQS delivery behavior&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.temporal.io/workflows" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Temporal workflows&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/#consumerconfigs" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Apache Kafka consumer concepts&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Status/429" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MDN: HTTP 429 Too Many Requests&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every Android app carries a lot of information about itself: who signed it, which permissions it asks for, which of its components other apps can reach, whether it is debuggable, which store installed it. Android exposes most of this through &lt;code&gt;PackageManager&lt;/code&gt;, but in pieces. The flags you need change with the API level, signing certificates have two different APIs, and a long app list can run into binder limits if you fetch it in one call.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;apk_inspector&lt;/code&gt; is a Flutter plugin that collects those pieces into typed Dart objects, for installed apps and for APK files on disk. It adds a plain-language permission catalog and a risk score whose formula is fully visible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What it does
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Listing apps.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;installedApps()&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;installedAppsPage()&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;installedAppsStream()&lt;/code&gt; return lightweight &lt;code&gt;AppSummary&lt;/code&gt; objects, sorted by package name and fetched in pages, so large devices do not block the UI or hit binder limits.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Icons.&lt;/strong&gt; PNG bytes drawn from the app's Drawable at the size you ask for, including adaptive icons drawn with the device mask. Load them lazily with &lt;code&gt;iconOf(packageName)&lt;/code&gt;, or inline with &lt;code&gt;includeIcons: true&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Details for one app.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;appDetails(packageName)&lt;/code&gt; returns an &lt;code&gt;AppDetails&lt;/code&gt; object with:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;version name, long version code, min, target and compile SDK;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;first install and last update time;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the installer, the initiating package and the update owner (&lt;code&gt;getInstallSourceInfo&lt;/code&gt; on API 30 and later, &lt;code&gt;getInstallerPackageName&lt;/code&gt; below);&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;flags: system, updatedSystem, debuggable, testOnly, allowBackup, usesCleartextTraffic, largeHeap, hasCode;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;sharedUserId, dataDir, sourceDir, split APK paths and total APK size;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;requested permissions with their granted state and protection level;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;activities, services, receivers and providers with their exported, permission and effective enabled state;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;signing certificates: current signers, past signers from key rotation and multiple signers on API 28 and later, and the older &lt;code&gt;GET_SIGNATURES&lt;/code&gt; below. Each certificate reports SHA-256, SHA-1, subject, issuer, validity, serial, signature algorithm and key algorithm.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;APK files.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;inspectApkFile(path)&lt;/code&gt; gives the same details for an APK on disk. It also reports which signature schemes (v1, v2, v3, v3.1) the file carries, read from the ZIP central directory and the APK Signing Block.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Pure-Dart helpers.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;PermissionCatalog&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;RiskScore&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;CertificateFingerprint&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;ApkSignatureSchemes&lt;/code&gt; work without the platform side.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;No permissions of its own.&lt;/strong&gt; The plugin manifest declares nothing: no permissions and no &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;queries&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How to use it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Add it from GitHub:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;dependencies&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;apk_inspector&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;git&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;https://github.com/AbdeldjalilChougui/apk_inspector&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The plugin's &lt;code&gt;minSdk&lt;/code&gt; is 21.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Package visibility comes first
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;From Android 11 (API 30), an app only sees the packages it declares. Without a declaration, &lt;code&gt;installedApps()&lt;/code&gt; returns little more than system packages and your own app, and &lt;code&gt;appDetails()&lt;/code&gt; fails with &lt;code&gt;notFound&lt;/code&gt; for anything else. The plugin declares nothing on purpose, so your app chooses:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A narrow &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;queries&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; element&lt;/strong&gt; in the manifest, listing the packages or intents you need. This is the preferred way.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, which sees everything. Google Play restricts this permission to apps whose core purpose needs the full list, such as device search, antivirus or app management tools, and you must justify it in the Play Console.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;inspectApkFile()&lt;/code&gt; needs no visibility declaration, but your app must be able to read the file path.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Dart
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight dart"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'package:apk_inspector/apk_inspector.dart'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;final&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;inspector&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ApkInspector&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Future&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;listApps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Streams page by page; icons are loaded separately.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;final&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;inspector&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;installedAppsStream&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;pageSize&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;50&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'${app.label} ${app.packageName} ${app.versionName}'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Future&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;details&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;String&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;packageName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;final&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;inspector&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;appDetails&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;packageName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'target ${d.targetSdk}, installer ${d.installSource?.installer}'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;final&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;signing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;currentSigners&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;CertificateInfo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'${cert.subject} ${cert.formattedSha256}'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;final&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;exportedComponents&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;where&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isProtected&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'open ${c.kind.name}: ${c.name}'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;final&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;risk&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;riskScore&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'risk ${risk.score}/100'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;risk&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;factors&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;forEach&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;on&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ApkInspectorException&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;catch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;code&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ApkInspectorErrorCode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;notFound&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Not installed or not visible to this app.'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Checking that an APK file is signed by a key you expect takes a few lines:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight dart"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Future&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;apkFile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;String&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;final&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;inspector&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;inspectApkFile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'${d.label} ${d.versionName} schemes: ${d.signingSchemes}'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pinned&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'6973d591ad0002d66bcc0d4a30b080bbaec4627dbeee67e59d9c91378176723f'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;final&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;signing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;currentSigners&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;any&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;CertificateFingerprint&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;matches&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sha256&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pinned&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'signed by the expected key: $ok'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


How the risk score works
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each requested permission falls into a bucket based only on its protection level: &lt;code&gt;normal&lt;/code&gt; is &lt;code&gt;low&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;dangerous&lt;/code&gt; is &lt;code&gt;high&lt;/code&gt;, signature-level permissions with the &lt;code&gt;appop&lt;/code&gt; flag are &lt;code&gt;specialAccess&lt;/code&gt;, the other signature-level permissions are &lt;code&gt;systemOnly&lt;/code&gt;, and anything not reported is &lt;code&gt;unknown&lt;/code&gt;. When the device reports no level, the plugin falls back to the AOSP level in &lt;code&gt;PermissionCatalog&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;RiskScore.of(details)&lt;/code&gt; then adds points, and the README gives the whole table:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;each permission: &lt;code&gt;high&lt;/code&gt; 6, &lt;code&gt;specialAccess&lt;/code&gt; 10, &lt;code&gt;systemOnly&lt;/code&gt; 2, &lt;code&gt;unknown&lt;/code&gt; 1, capped at 50 in total;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;debuggable&lt;/code&gt; 15, &lt;code&gt;testOnly&lt;/code&gt; 5, &lt;code&gt;usesCleartextTraffic&lt;/code&gt; 5, &lt;code&gt;allowBackup&lt;/code&gt; 3;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;targetSdk&lt;/code&gt; below 23: 10;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;each exported component without a permission: 1, capped at 15;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;sharedUserId&lt;/code&gt; set: 2.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The sum is clamped to 0..100, and every point appears in &lt;code&gt;RiskReport.factors&lt;/code&gt;. The README is clear about what this number is: a heuristic for sorting and triage, not a malware verdict. Requested permissions count whether or not they are granted, so APK files and installed apps score the same way.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Things to know
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Android only.&lt;/strong&gt; On other platforms every call throws &lt;code&gt;ApkInspectorException&lt;/code&gt; with the code &lt;code&gt;unavailable&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Privacy.&lt;/strong&gt; The list of installed apps is personal data: it can reveal health conditions, religion, finances or dating habits. The plugin reads package data only on the device and sends nothing anywhere. If your app uploads what it collects, tell users, get consent where the law requires it, and complete the Play Data safety form.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;exported&lt;/code&gt; is what PackageManager resolved.&lt;/strong&gt; The plugin does not parse the binary manifest, so it cannot tell an explicit &lt;code&gt;android:exported&lt;/code&gt; from a default, and intent filters are not reported.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Scheme detection is not verification.&lt;/strong&gt; It reports which signature schemes are present. It does not verify signatures. ZIP64 archives and v4 &lt;code&gt;.idsig&lt;/code&gt; files are not handled.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;APK files have less data.&lt;/strong&gt; Grant states, install times, installer and dataDir are null, and rotation history depends on the device's parser.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Some fields need newer devices.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;minSdk&lt;/code&gt; needs Android 7.0 or later and &lt;code&gt;compileSdk&lt;/code&gt; needs Android 12 or later on the device running the plugin. &lt;code&gt;originatingPackage&lt;/code&gt; is only revealed to callers with &lt;code&gt;INSTALL_PACKAGES&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;usesCleartextTraffic&lt;/code&gt; is the manifest flag.&lt;/strong&gt; A network security config can refine it per domain.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Where the catalog comes from.&lt;/strong&gt; Protection levels for the 1016 permissions in &lt;code&gt;PermissionCatalog&lt;/code&gt; are generated from the AOSP platform manifest at a pinned commit (Apache License 2.0). The descriptions and groups were written for this package.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What was tested.&lt;/strong&gt; The example app has an emulator integration test. It compares the installed-app list with &lt;code&gt;pm list packages&lt;/code&gt;, and compares the app's details and a copied debug APK with &lt;code&gt;apksigner verify --print-certs&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;aapt2 dump badging&lt;/code&gt; run on the host. It was tested on the Android emulator (Pixel 10 system image, API 37). It has not yet been tested on a physical phone, OEM system apps or split APKs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why I built it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reading app metadata on Android looks like one call and turns out to be many. Signing data alone needs &lt;code&gt;GET_SIGNING_CERTIFICATES&lt;/code&gt; on newer versions and &lt;code&gt;GET_SIGNATURES&lt;/code&gt; on older ones, with key rotation in between. The install source has a new API from Android 11. A full app list has to be paged. And a permission name like &lt;code&gt;SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW&lt;/code&gt; says little to a user until someone explains it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wanted all of that in one typed API, with the same shape for an installed app and for an APK file. I also wanted the risk number to be honest: a published formula where every point can be traced to a factor, instead of a score nobody can check. And because the list of installed apps is sensitive, the plugin declares no permissions and leaves the visibility choice to the app that uses it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Get the code
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The source, the full formula and data sources, the example app and the tests are on GitHub: &lt;a href="https://github.com/AbdeldjalilChougui/apk_inspector" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/AbdeldjalilChougui/apk_inspector&lt;/a&gt;. It's MIT licensed; the generated protection-level table carries the Apache-2.0 notice of its AOSP source. Issues and pull requests are welcome.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published at &lt;a href="https://abdeldjalilchougui.biz/blog/apk-inspector" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;abdeldjalilchougui.biz&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An invoice workflow can fail in two different ways: it can extract the wrong value, or it can confidently move a correct-looking value into the wrong business action. I designed this n8n demo to make those steps visible and keep approval outside the automation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The example processes Arabic or English invoices supplied as PDF, JPG, or PNG. It uses Gemini to extract fields, then applies deterministic checks to catch arithmetic inconsistencies before writing a record to an n8n Data Table. Each record remains pending review.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The flow
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Receive the file.&lt;/strong&gt; The workflow accepts an invoice document and routes supported formats into extraction.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Extract candidate fields.&lt;/strong&gt; Gemini returns structured values such as supplier, invoice number, line amounts, tax, and total. These are suggestions from a model, not trusted accounting data.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Check arithmetic in code.&lt;/strong&gt; The workflow recalculates totals and compares them with the extracted total. A mismatch is recorded as a validation issue rather than silently accepted.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Look for duplicates.&lt;/strong&gt; It checks the supplier and invoice number against prior records. This catches a common repeat-submission case, though it is not a universal duplicate detector.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Save for review.&lt;/strong&gt; The result is written to an n8n Data Table with a pending-review state so a person can inspect the document and extracted values.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why keep approval separate?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Extraction quality and business authorization are different questions. A model can help turn an unstructured document into fields, and code can check arithmetic, but neither decides whether a payment should be approved. Keeping the demo at “pending review” makes that boundary explicit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same separation is useful when tailoring an invoice process for a business: define what the system may extract, which checks can be deterministic, who reviews exceptions, and what authenticated downstream action is permitted. A production integration would also need the customer’s document storage, access rules, accounting system, retention policy, and error-handling requirements. Those are not included here.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I verified
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The repository includes two workflow exports, six screenshots, synthetic invoice examples, and 26 passing offline checks. Those checks do not establish a fresh live n8n run or production readiness. The public exports need credentials, the supporting subworkflow and Data Tables to be configured, and fresh runtime testing; the backup Gemini model also needs review. The demo does not approve invoices, post to an ERP, or claim zero errors or measured savings.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Source and setup notes: &lt;a href="https://github.com/ahmednegm91/intelligent-invoice-processing" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/ahmednegm91/intelligent-invoice-processing&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a portfolio demonstration of an approach I can adapt after requirements and approval boundaries are clear.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;2026 is not asking you to be louder. It’s asking you to be more deliberate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The last few years rewarded motion: shipping fast, reacting faster, staying visible. But the next era belongs to people who understand something more powerful, but subtle. That leverage beats effort, and clarity beats speed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My projection is that most people will enter 2026 busy but very few will enter it positioned.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This article is an invitation to be in the second group. I will show you 5 ways you can approach this.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Activity is no longer the scarce resource. Agency is.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Everyone is doing something. Meetings. Posts. Roadmaps. Side projects. Opinions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The rare factor is &lt;strong&gt;agency&lt;/strong&gt;, which is defined the ability to decide what not to do, to shape outcomes instead of responding to them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Agency begins when you stop asking: &lt;em&gt;“What should I work on next?”&lt;/em&gt; And start asking:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;“What, if done once, makes ten other things unnecessary?”&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In product, this is the difference between shipping features and changing the system that decides which features exist.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
In life, it’s the difference between grinding harder and changing your position on the board.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Leverage compounds. Effort exhausts.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Effort has diminishing returns. Leverage has non-linear ones.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In product, leverage looks like:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Building platforms instead of features&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Investing in primitives instead of workflows&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Making one irreversible decision that removes ten future decisions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Outside of work, leverage looks like:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Relationships that unlock rooms you can’t brute-force into&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Writing once and letting it work for you for years&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Learning skills that move with you, not roles that trap you&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If 2025 was about doing more, 2026 should be about making fewer things matter more.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!r60e!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F968d8121-cc38-4d12-ab40-646b17dd9d73_1536x1024.png" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fhoipsl4sprtvgnajpfcy.png" width="800" height="534"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. Stop optimizing for optionality. Start optimizing for trajectory.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Optionality feels safe. It’s also how you stay average.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Many smart people delay commitment under the banner of “keeping doors open.” But doors don’t stay open forever, they close quietly when you don’t walk through them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I have observed that trajectory beats optionality.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In product terms:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A clear bet with conviction outperforms a flexible roadmap with no spine.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A strong point of view attracts talent, customers, and trust.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In life:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;People follow direction, and naturally ignore hesitation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Momentum prefers decisiveness, even imperfect ones.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You don’t need perfect information. You need a direction you are willing to defend, or at best learn from.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;4. Relationships are not a soft skill. They are the system.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It has repeatedly been proven that most outcomes that matter in tech do not come from raw brilliance. They come from &lt;strong&gt;trust, timing, and proximity&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The most effective PMs and leaders:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Are remembered when opportunities arise&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Are trusted with ambiguity&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Are invited into conversations before decisions are made&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As much as it is widely marketed, this doesn’t come from networking harder, but from &lt;strong&gt;being useful, consistent, and principled over time&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In 2026, invest in:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Fewer relationships with depth&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Long-term collaborators rather than transactional ones&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Being the person others want in the room when stakes are high&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your network is less of your audience but your leverage graph.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;5. Action without reflection is just motion. Reflection without action is indulgence.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As you execute, remember that the goal isn’t to slow down. It is to &lt;strong&gt;aim better&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;High-leverage people operate in cycles:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pause → decide → act → learn&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Not react → scramble → burn out → repeat&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In product, we call this judgment. In other spheres, it is referred to as maturity.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before you rush into 2026, ask:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What am I still doing out of habit, not impact?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Where am I mistaking activity for progress?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Which one or two bets would meaningfully change my next three years?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then act, decisively. We must also remember to move with intensity, consistency and discipline&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!eRK9!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa59e992c-4a26-436f-8ebe-f8bde0f49341_1536x1024.png" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fz2ekyztxuiqj3gnevclo.png" width="800" height="534"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;My Bet is that 2026 will reward people who:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Choose leverage over effort&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Direction over motion&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Relationships over reach&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Agency over activity&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You don’t need a reinvention. You need a &lt;strong&gt;position&lt;/strong&gt;. Choose it carefully.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This is part 2 of 8 of the free series &lt;a href="https://tt-tech.io/series/frontend-system-design" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Frontend system design for enterprise applications&lt;/a&gt;, first published on &lt;a href="https://tt-tech.io/blog/enterprise-frontend-state-ownership" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;tt-tech.io&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A support agent opens the link a customer sent: an account page, filtered on one category. The page shows all categories, because the filter lived in a component field and not in the URL. Nobody wrote a bug. The state was stored as the wrong kind.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is the state problem in enterprise apps. The library choice comes last. First you decide what kind each piece of state is, who owns it, and how long it lives.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Five kinds of state
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Server data.&lt;/strong&gt; Accounts, transactions. The backend owns it; the frontend holds a copy that can be stale (part 3).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;UI state.&lt;/strong&gt; Which panel is open. Owned by one component, gone when it is gone.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Form state.&lt;/strong&gt; A draft, its validation, whether it is dirty.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;URL state.&lt;/strong&gt; Filters, tabs, the selected record. Anything a link, a support ticket or the back button must restore.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Session state.&lt;/strong&gt; The user and their roles. Truly global.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A common state bug is one kind stored as another: server data copied into a component, a filter in memory that belongs in the URL, roles cached in a store nobody refreshes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F48ffean7v511fa2fsdds.webp" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F48ffean7v511fa2fsdds.webp" alt="Five kinds of state, each with an example on Atlas Bank, its owner, where it lives and how long: server data such as accounts lives in AccountsStore until it is stale; UI state lives in a component signal until the component is gone; form state lives in the form model until submit or cancel; URL state such as the account id and the filter lives in the URL as long as the link; session state, the user and their roles, lives in one global store until logout. A common bug is one kind stored as another" width="799" height="358"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The URL is state
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If a person might share it, bookmark it or go back to it, it belongs in the URL. In Angular, &lt;code&gt;withComponentInputBinding&lt;/code&gt; binds route parameters to component inputs, and by default the router sets an input to &lt;code&gt;undefined&lt;/code&gt; when the value is missing from the route, which "ensures that stale data is not retained".&lt;a href="https://angular.dev/guide/routing/common-router-tasks" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;[1]&lt;/a&gt; Query parameters bind the same way, so a filter can live there too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


One store per domain, one global store at most
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I would give each domain its own store: accounts, cards, requests. A team owns its store the way it owns its library. A single global store looks tidy on day one and turns into the place where every team's state meets every other team's bugs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What stays global is what is cross-cutting by nature: the session, the permissions, maybe feature flags. Everything else stays close to the feature that owns it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Derive, do not duplicate
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A total balance is not state. It is a function of the accounts. Angular says it directly: "Avoid using effects for propagation of state changes", use computed signals instead.&lt;a href="https://angular.dev/guide/signals/effect" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;[2]&lt;/a&gt; React says the same in its own words: "When something can be calculated from the existing props or state, don't put it in state. Instead, calculate it during rendering."&lt;a href="https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;[3]&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fcuvdjk541qsb2m7e4xm1.webp" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fcuvdjk541qsb2m7e4xm1.webp" alt="Derived against duplicated. Two open accounts of 12 400,00 MAD and 30 000,00 MAD give a total balance of 42 400,00 MAD. After a transfer of 500,00 MAD, the derived total, a computed signal, follows to 41 900,00 MAD and every screen shows the same number. A duplicated total, set once or by an effect, stays at 42 400,00 MAD, so two owners disagree" width="800" height="358"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Every copy of a value is a second owner. Two owners will disagree.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Long regulated forms
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A loan application or a patient intake form is state with rules: drafts that survive a lunch break, validation that mirrors the server's, and dirty tracking so nobody loses an hour of input. The server still validates; the client check is for the person typing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For new code, Angular's Signal Forms sync the form model with its fields automatically. The Angular team is careful here: they "work best in new applications built with signals", and "reactive forms remain a solid choice" in an existing app that uses them.&lt;a href="https://angular.dev/guide/forms/signals/overview" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;[4]&lt;/a&gt; In a ten-year codebase, that is the right advice. Do not migrate forms for fashion.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Signals or RxJS
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My rule: signals for state (a value now), RxJS for events over time (keystrokes, websockets, retries). The bridge exists: &lt;code&gt;toSignal&lt;/code&gt; turns an Observable into a signal and "behaves similarly to the async pipe in templates".&lt;a href="https://angular.dev/ecosystem/rxjs-interop" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;[5]&lt;/a&gt; Signals also fit zoneless Angular, where "updating a signal that's read in a template" is one of the notifications that schedules change detection.&lt;a href="https://angular.dev/guide/zoneless" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;[6]&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


On Atlas Bank
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;AccountsStore&lt;/code&gt; in &lt;code&gt;libs/data-access/banking&lt;/code&gt; keeps its state private and hands out read-only signals and derived values:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;  &lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;accountsState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Account&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;transactionsState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Transaction&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;beneficiariesState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Beneficiary&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;loadingState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;loadedState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;inFlight&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;accounts&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;accountsState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;asReadonly&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;transactions&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;transactionsState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;asReadonly&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;beneficiaries&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;beneficiariesState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;asReadonly&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;loading&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;loadingState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;asReadonly&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;loaded&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;loadedState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;asReadonly&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;totalBalanceMad&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;computed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kc"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;accountsState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'closed'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;reduce&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sum&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sum&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;balanceMad&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;asReadonly()&lt;/code&gt; exposes a value "without allowing them to modify it directly",&lt;a href="https://angular.dev/guide/signals" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;[7]&lt;/a&gt; so the store is the only owner. The account detail page gets its &lt;code&gt;id&lt;/code&gt; from the route through &lt;code&gt;withComponentInputBinding&lt;/code&gt; and derives the filtered list with &lt;code&gt;computed&lt;/code&gt;. Its category filter, though, is a component signal. Fine for a demo; in a bank's support flow I would move it to a query parameter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The legacy transfer screen shows the old shape: plain fields, manual subscriptions, and &lt;code&gt;markForCheck()&lt;/code&gt; after them. The repo's &lt;code&gt;AGENTS.md&lt;/code&gt; calls that "a smell to migrate, not a pattern to copy".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I left out, and why
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Redux, MobX, Zustand, NgRx comparisons.&lt;/strong&gt; A library tour. Ownership decides first; any of them can hold the result.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Immutability as a concept.&lt;/strong&gt; Implied by read-only signals and derived values.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Event sourcing on the client.&lt;/strong&gt; Rare in a frontend, a backend shape.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Atomic state.&lt;/strong&gt; React library vocabulary for the same idea as "derive, do not duplicate".&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


On Monday
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pick one screen and label every piece of state with its kind: server, UI, form, URL, session.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Find one value stored twice. Make one of them a &lt;code&gt;computed&lt;/code&gt; (or a calculation during render).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Find one filter that a support ticket cannot reproduce. Move it to the URL.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://tt-tech.io/blog/enterprise-frontend-networking-caching" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Part 3, on networking and caching&lt;/a&gt;, is next.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


For your coding agent
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The rules of this article, written for an agent. Paste them into your &lt;code&gt;AGENTS.md&lt;/code&gt;, save them as a skill, or point your agent at the file.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight markdown"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="gu"&gt;# State rules for enterprise apps
&lt;/span&gt;
Source: https://tt-tech.io/blog/enterprise-frontend-state-ownership
For Angular and React frontends. Add to AGENTS.md or save as a skill. Your project's own rules win where they differ.

&lt;span class="gu"&gt;## Rules
&lt;/span&gt;
1. Before you add state, name its kind: server data, UI state, form state, URL state or session state. Store it as that kind.
2. Put anything a link, a bookmark, a support ticket or the back button must restore in the URL: filters, tabs, the selected record. In Angular, bind route and query parameters to inputs with &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`withComponentInputBinding`&lt;/span&gt;, and expect a missing parameter to arrive as &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`undefined`&lt;/span&gt;.
3. Server data belongs to the backend. Never copy it into a component. Read it from the store of its domain.
4. Give each domain its own store (accounts, cards, requests), owned by the team that owns the domain. Never add a single global store for everything.
5. Keep global only what is cross-cutting by nature: the session, the permissions, maybe feature flags. Do not cache roles in a store nobody refreshes.
6. Keep UI state (which panel is open) in the component that owns it.
7. Derive, do not duplicate. If a value can be calculated from state, use &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`computed`&lt;/span&gt; in Angular or calculate it during render in React. Never store it as a second copy.
8. Never use &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`effect`&lt;/span&gt; to propagate state changes. Use &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`computed`&lt;/span&gt; instead.
9. Keep a store's writable state private. Expose read-only signals with &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`asReadonly()`&lt;/span&gt; and derived values, so the store is the only owner.
10. In long regulated forms, keep drafts, mirror the server's validation and track dirty state. The server still validates; the client check is for the person typing.
11. Use Signal Forms in new code built with signals. Keep reactive forms in an existing app that uses them. Do not migrate forms for fashion.
12. Use signals for state (a value now) and RxJS for events over time (keystrokes, websockets, retries). Bridge them with &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`toSignal`&lt;/span&gt;.

&lt;span class="gu"&gt;## Before you say done
&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;- &lt;/span&gt;For every new piece of state in the diff, list its kind and its owner in the pull request.
&lt;span class="k"&gt;- &lt;/span&gt;Search the diff for &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`effect(`&lt;/span&gt; calls that set other state, and for stored values that could be a &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`computed`&lt;/span&gt; or a calculation during render.
&lt;span class="k"&gt;- &lt;/span&gt;For every new filter, tab or selection, confirm it lives in the URL, or say why not.
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The same rules as a file: &lt;a href="https://tt-tech.io/agents/enterprise-frontend-state-ownership.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://tt-tech.io/agents/enterprise-frontend-state-ownership.md&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Sources
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Angular, &lt;a href="https://angular.dev/guide/routing/common-router-tasks" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Other routing tasks&lt;/a&gt;, docs&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Angular, &lt;a href="https://angular.dev/guide/signals/effect" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Side effects for non-reactives APIs&lt;/a&gt;, docs&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;React, &lt;a href="https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;You Might Not Need an Effect&lt;/a&gt;, docs&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Angular, &lt;a href="https://angular.dev/guide/forms/signals/overview" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Forms with Angular Signals&lt;/a&gt;, docs&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Angular, &lt;a href="https://angular.dev/ecosystem/rxjs-interop" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;RxJS interop with Angular signals&lt;/a&gt;, docs&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Angular, &lt;a href="https://angular.dev/guide/zoneless" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Angular without ZoneJS (Zoneless)&lt;/a&gt;, docs&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Angular, &lt;a href="https://angular.dev/guide/signals" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Angular Signals&lt;/a&gt;, docs&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Part 2 of 8 of the free series &lt;a href="https://tt-tech.io/series/frontend-system-design" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Frontend system design for enterprise applications&lt;/a&gt; on tt-tech.io, also in French and Arabic.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Have you ever noticed how some websites reveal their content gradually as you scroll down the page?&lt;br&gt;
Instead of displaying all elements at once, headings, buttons, images, and other elements appear with smooth animations.&lt;br&gt;
This technique is often called Staggered Animations.&lt;br&gt;
In this tutorial, I'll show you how to create this effect in a React application using a lightweight JavaScript library called AOS (Animate On Scroll).&lt;br&gt;
I'll use a real example from my Cosmetics website to demonstrate how different elements can appear with different animation delays.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What Is AOS?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AOS stands for Animate On Scroll.&lt;br&gt;
It's a JavaScript library that allows developers to animate elements when they enter the viewport.&lt;br&gt;
Instead of implementing custom scroll event listeners, we can simply add HTML data attributes to the elements we want to animate.&lt;br&gt;
For example:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;jsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;aos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"fade-up"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Welcome&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;to&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Our&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Website&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;


&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This makes the heading appear with a fade-up animation when it enters the viewport.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Installing AOS in React&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;First, install the library:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;bash
npm install aos
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Next, import AOS and its CSS file into your React application:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;jsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'react'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Aos&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'aos'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'aos/dist/aos.css'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Then initialize the library inside your main component:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;jsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Aos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;init&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;duration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Let's understand the configuration:&lt;br&gt;
duration: 1000 sets the animation duration to 1000 milliseconds.&lt;br&gt;
Aos.init() initializes the animation library.&lt;br&gt;
useEffect() runs the initialization after the component mounts.&lt;br&gt;
[] ensures the effect runs only once per mount.&lt;br&gt;
Now AOS is ready to use throughout the application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Understanding Staggered Animations&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;br&gt;
One interesting feature of AOS is the ability to control when individual elements start their animations.&lt;br&gt;
We can achieve this using:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;jsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;aos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;delay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"500"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The delay value is measured in milliseconds.&lt;br&gt;
For example:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;jsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;aos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"fade-up"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;aos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;delay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"200"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;First&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Element&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;aos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"fade-up"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;aos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;delay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"400"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Second&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Element&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;aos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"fade-up"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;aos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;delay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"600"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Third&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Element&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Each element has the same animation, but a different delay.&lt;br&gt;
This creates a sequential visual effect rather than making all elements animate simultaneously.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A Real Example from My React Project&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;br&gt;
In my Cosmetics website, I used this technique inside the Hero section.&lt;br&gt;
The goal was to make the content appear gradually and create a more engaging first impression.&lt;br&gt;
Here is a simplified version of the implementation:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;jsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Hero&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;section&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;hgroup&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;aos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"fade-up"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;aos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;delay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"500"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Enhance&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Your&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Natural&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Beauty&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Discover&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;beauty&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;and&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;skincare&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;treatments&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;hgroup&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;nav&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;aos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"fade-up"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;aos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;delay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"600"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;href&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"#appointment"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Book&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Consultation&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;href&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"#services"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Explore&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Services&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;nav&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;aside&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;aos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"fade-up"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;aos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;delay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"800"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;500&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Happy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Clients&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;15&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Specialists&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;50&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Treatments&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Years&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;aside&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;figure&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;aos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"fade-up"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;aos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;delay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"700"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;img&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;src&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/images/hero.png"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;alt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Cosmetic treatment"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;figure&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;section&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Notice that I didn't apply the animation to every individual element.&lt;br&gt;
Instead, I applied it to groups of related elements using semantic HTML elements such as hgroup, nav, aside, and figure.&lt;br&gt;
This keeps the JSX cleaner and makes the animation behavior easier to manage.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Analyzing the Animation Delays&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;br&gt;
Let's look at the actual delay values used in my project:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Heading &amp;amp; Description: fade-up — 500ms&lt;br&gt;
Navigation Buttons: fade-up — 600ms&lt;br&gt;
Hero Image: fade-up — 700ms&lt;br&gt;
Statistics: fade-up — 800ms&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;All four elements use the same animation, but their delays create a layered visual experience.&lt;br&gt;
One important detail: the delays are relative to when each element's animation is triggered, so the actual appearance sequence can also depend on when each element enters the viewport.&lt;br&gt;
This gives developers more control over how content is revealed while scrolling.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Other Animation Effects You Can Use&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;br&gt;
AOS provides several animation types beyond fade-up.&lt;br&gt;
Here are a few examples:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;jsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;aos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"fade-up"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Fade&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Up&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;aos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"fade-right"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Fade&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Right&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;aos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"zoom-in"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Zoom&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;In&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;aos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"flip-left"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Flip&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Left&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;You can experiment with different effects depending on the design of your website.&lt;br&gt;
For example, fade animations work well for text, while zoom animations can be useful for images and visual cards.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Some Tips from My Experience&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;br&gt;
After working with AOS in my React project, here are a few things I recommend:&lt;br&gt;
Don't animate every element. Too many animations can make the interface distracting.&lt;br&gt;
Use delays carefully to create a natural visual flow.&lt;br&gt;
Keep animation durations reasonable.&lt;br&gt;
Test your animations on mobile devices.&lt;br&gt;
Consider users who prefer reduced motion.&lt;br&gt;
Animations should improve the user experience, not slow down the interaction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Final Thoughts&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;br&gt;
Adding scroll animations to a React application doesn't have to be complicated.&lt;br&gt;
With AOS, we can create engaging visual effects using simple data attributes without writing custom scroll detection logic.&lt;br&gt;
The most interesting part is not simply adding animations, but controlling their timing to create a more natural and enjoyable browsing experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Do you prefer using animation libraries like AOS, or do you prefer implementing animations with CSS?&lt;br&gt;
Share your thoughts in the comments!&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Microsoft Publisher is being discontinued at the end of October 2026. For years I used it for a hobby: designing transit network maps, the kind with colored lines, 45° bends and round stop symbols. I don’t need InDesign for that, and I couldn’t find a simple replacement. So I built one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It’s called Liniennetz-Editor, it’s free and open source (MIT), and it runs completely in the browser with no backend. Here’s how it works and what turned out to be tricky.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The idea: Describe, not draw
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Graphics programs make you draw every line segment. For a network with 50 lines and 300 stations that is miserable. The editor flips this around:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Describe the network: stops, lines, categories (e.g. “frequent” vs. “rare”), which line serves which stops&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Place the stops on a grid&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The program computes everything else: connections, corners, parallel lanes, stop symbols, key&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The result is a modern-looking map that only uses 0°, 45° and 90°, exported as SVG, PNG or PDF.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Stack
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;React and Fluent UI for the surrounding interface&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Every edit clones the project state, which gives undo/redo almost for free&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The map itself is plain SVG generated as a string in a separate engine module with no UI dependency&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Projects are saved as JSON files, no server involved&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keeping the engine separate from the UI was the best early decision. The same function renders the interactive canvas and the export.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


1. Routing between two grid points
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Between two stops on a grid there are three cases: same row or column, exactly diagonal, or neither. The third case is the interesting one. You need a diagonal part of length &lt;code&gt;min(|dx|, |dy|)&lt;/code&gt; plus a straight part for the rest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Where the straight part goes matters visually. Putting it all at one end looks clumsy, so I split it between both ends and let the diagonal sit in the middle:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;route&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ax&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;abs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;dx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ay&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;abs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;dy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ax&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ay&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ax&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;      &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// straight or pure diagonal&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ax&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;                      &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// diagonal length&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;abs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ax&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;                     &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// straight remainder&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;n1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;ceil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;                      &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// straight part at the start&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sign&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;dx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sign&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;dy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;horizontal&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ax&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;p1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;horizontal&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;n1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;horizontal&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;n1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;p2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;p1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;p1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;p1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;p2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The real version also handles odd remainders (a flip toggle lets the user choose which end gets the larger part) and removes degenerate points.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One design rule matters a lot: routes are computed per edge, not per line. If two lines share a section, they automatically share the same geometry, which makes bundling possible later.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


2. Rounded corners
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Corners are replaced with circular arcs. The tangent length depends on the turning angle, and the radius is clamped so arcs never overlap on short segments:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;fillet&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;v&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;l1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;hypot&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;v&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;v&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;l2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;hypot&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;v&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;v&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;u1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;v&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;l1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;v&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;l1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;u2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;v&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;l2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;v&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;l2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;theta&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;acos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;u1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;u2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;u1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;u2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// turning angle&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;k&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;tan&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;theta&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;t&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;k&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;l1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;l2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;           &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// tangent length, clamped&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rr&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;t&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;k&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;                                    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// effective radius&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sweep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;u1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;u2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;u1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;u2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`L${v.x - u1.x * t} ${v.y - u1.y * t}`&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`A${rr} ${rr} 0 0 ${sweep} ${v.x + u2.x * t} ${v.y + u2.y * t}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That string goes straight into an SVG &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;path d="..."&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


3. The hard part: bundling parallel lines
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When three lines run along the same track, they should appear as three parallel strokes with a constant gap, including through bends.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My approach:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Break every line into unit grid steps. Each step gets a key (its two endpoints).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;For each step, collect all lines that use it. Each line’s lane is its index in that list, centered: &lt;code&gt;offset = (index - (count - 1) / 2) * gap&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;At every vertex, compute the offset path’s corner by intersecting the two offset lines. That keeps the gap constant through a bend.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;For radii, give the innermost line the base radius and let the outer lines run concentrically around it, so strokes don’t cross inside a curve.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The nastiest bug class here is lane order. If two lines share a stretch, they must keep the same relative order along the whole shared run, otherwise they cross each other for no reason. The engine decides the order once per contiguous shared run, using the geometry (which side each line turns toward) as the tiebreaker. Users can override the order per connection by clicking it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Where a lane count changes (a line joins or leaves a bundle), the path needs a 45° shift so the gap is preserved. Currently the whole bundle re-centers when that happens, which creates a visible kink in otherwise straight lines. A Reddit user rightly pointed out that straightness should beat symmetry here, so that’s next on my list.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


4. Stop symbols
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A stop served by one line is a circle. A stop served by several parallel lines becomes a pill spanning all lanes, oriented across the bundle direction (including diagonals). If lines cross at different angles, the symbol turns into a rounded rectangle. Stops where only some lines call get a gap in the pill, drawn as separate parts joined by a thin connector.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


5. Performance
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With about 300 stops and 50 lines, recomputing everything on each mouse move would be slow. A cheap fix works well: the engine builds a cache key from the relevant parts of the state and returns the previous result if nothing that affects geometry has changed. Dragging a label doesn’t recompute the routing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


What I’d do differently
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Add automated tests for the geometry earlier. I verified the routing visually, which doesn’t scale.&lt;br&gt;
Decide on the data model for lane order from day one instead of retrofitting manual overrides.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Example map: Ostthüringen regional rail, Germany
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fh08it8qkh2pr23ww87mf.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fh08it8qkh2pr23ww87mf.png" alt="Ostthüringen regional rail transit map" width="800" height="800"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Try it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Editor and examples: &lt;a href="https://transitmapper.github.io/liniennetzeditor/en/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://transitmapper.github.io/liniennetzeditor/en/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Source (MIT): &lt;a href="https://github.com/transitmapper/liniennetzeditor" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/transitmapper/liniennetzeditor&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The interface is German only for now. If you make transit maps, or just like grid geometry, I’d love feedback. What’s missing? What’s confusing?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The symptom, measured
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A site with different content on every route. I measured the raw HTML of 15 URLs — 7 static routes plus the 8 dynamic routes of one community — and this is what the server returns:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;route&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;title served&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;/&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;El Arquitecto Cuántico — Libros de Jose Alexis Román Martínez&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;/comunidad&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;El Arquitecto Cuántico — Libros de Jose Alexis Román Martínez&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;/comunidad/arquitecto-cuantico&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;El Arquitecto Cuántico — Libros de Jose Alexis Román Martínez&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;/comunidad/codigo-origen&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;El Arquitecto Cuántico — Libros de Jose Alexis Román Martínez&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;/comunidad/mente-eterna&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;El Arquitecto Cuántico — Libros de Jose Alexis Román Martínez&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;/privacy&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;El Arquitecto Cuántico — Libros de Jose Alexis Román Martínez&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;/terms&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;El Arquitecto Cuántico — Libros de Jose Alexis Román Martínez&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;/informes/ariastate&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Informe de riesgo estructural — ariastate · Platino Labs&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fourteen of fifteen. The exception is not random, and that is the whole piece.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;(Abbreviated table: 8 of the 15 measured URLs are listed. The remaining 7 — &lt;code&gt;/pinterest-demo&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;/tiktok-demo&lt;/code&gt; and the 5 community slugs not listed — return the same root title. The full listing is reproduced at the end.)&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Also: &lt;code&gt;rel="canonical"&lt;/code&gt; appears &lt;strong&gt;0 times&lt;/strong&gt; across the 15. And &lt;code&gt;https://arquitectocuantico.com/sitemap.xml&lt;/code&gt; answers &lt;strong&gt;HTTP 404&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The cause is not the content
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is not that the site does not define titles. It is that it cannot define them with the mechanism it chose.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In the Next.js App Router there are two ways to set a title. The first is exporting &lt;code&gt;metadata&lt;/code&gt; from a server component: the framework serialises it into &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;head&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;. The second is rendering a &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;title&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; element anywhere in the tree — React 19 hoists it into &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;head&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;, during server rendering too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;"use client"&lt;/code&gt; prevents neither of those things by itself&lt;/strong&gt;, and it is worth saying so before going on, because the usual intuition — "client means the browser paints it and the server does not" — is false, and it is measured with one command. &lt;code&gt;/privacy&lt;/code&gt; is a client component and its full text is in the raw HTML:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;curl -sL https://arquitectocuantico.com/privacy &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; sed &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'s/&amp;lt;[^&amp;gt;]*&amp;gt;/ /g'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; tr -s &lt;span class="s1"&gt;' '&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; wc -c
→ 3194
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Three thousand one hundred and ninety-four characters of visible text served by the server, with JavaScript never executed. The client component is prerendered all the same.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What &lt;code&gt;"use client"&lt;/code&gt; does do is exclude the module from the &lt;code&gt;metadata&lt;/code&gt; export: that API is server-side and is not available on the client. The site chose &lt;code&gt;metadata&lt;/code&gt; — once, in &lt;code&gt;src/app/layout.tsx&lt;/code&gt; — and marked the other seven routes as &lt;code&gt;"use client"&lt;/code&gt;. The result is not an impossibility: it is an omission, with one declaration for fifteen URLs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Counting over the repository, at &lt;code&gt;fb28bab&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;find src/app -name page.tsx &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; wc -l                            → 8
grep -rl &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'^"use client"'&lt;/span&gt; src/app --include=page.tsx &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; wc -l    → 7
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Seven of eight &lt;code&gt;page.tsx&lt;/code&gt; open with &lt;code&gt;"use client"&lt;/code&gt; — 87.5%. The only one that does not is &lt;code&gt;src/app/informes/ariastate/page.tsx&lt;/code&gt;, and it is the only one that exports a title of its own.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The 2×2, without exceptions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;what the component is&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;URLs served&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;declares a title of its own&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;"use client"&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;14&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;server&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Zero of fourteen, one of one. The only route that declares anything is also the only one &lt;code&gt;metadata&lt;/code&gt; is allowed in.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And the effect is multiplicative, not additive: every new slug added to the community inherits the same root title without anyone touching anything. The debt does not grow one at a time; it grows with the catalogue.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The fix is structural
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Writing a &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;title&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; inside each client component would also work — we just measured that it is prerendered — and that is precisely the trap: it scatters the same declaration across fourteen files, one per slug, and the fifteenth added tomorrow forgets it again. The debt is not paid once; it is paid with the catalogue.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix moves the declaration up to where the server lives, which is where the catalogue is already listed:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;src/app/layout.tsx&lt;/code&gt; — &lt;code&gt;metadataBase&lt;/code&gt;, title template, &lt;code&gt;openGraph&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;robots&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;src/app/comunidad/layout.tsx&lt;/code&gt; — the title of the shared room.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;src/app/comunidad/[slug]/layout.tsx&lt;/code&gt; — &lt;code&gt;generateMetadata&lt;/code&gt;, which does receive the &lt;code&gt;params&lt;/code&gt; and produces a title per slug.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;src/app/sitemap.ts&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;src/app/robots.ts&lt;/code&gt; — the two routes that did not exist and answered 404.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;next.config.ts&lt;/code&gt; — &lt;code&gt;X-Robots-Tag&lt;/code&gt; headers so the two demos are not indexed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Six files, +147 −2 lines. With that: &lt;code&gt;tsc --noEmit&lt;/code&gt; → RC 0, &lt;code&gt;next build&lt;/code&gt; → RC 0, and the three server layouts emit a canonical plus three &lt;code&gt;hreflang&lt;/code&gt; alternates per page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The blind spot: the fix does not reach these URLs
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is where the piece parts ways with almost everything written about this topic. The patch is written and validated. &lt;strong&gt;And none of the fifteen URLs serves it.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;$ curl -s -o /dev/null -w &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'%{http_code} age=%header{age}\n'&lt;/span&gt; https://arquitectocuantico.com/
200 &lt;span class="nv"&gt;age&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;783014

$ curl -s -o /dev/null -w &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'%{http_code} age=%header{age}\n'&lt;/span&gt; https://arquitectocuantico.com/sitemap.xml
404 &lt;span class="nv"&gt;age&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;175297
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;783014&lt;/code&gt; seconds is &lt;strong&gt;9.06 days&lt;/strong&gt;: the HTML the front page serves today was generated before the patch existed. &lt;code&gt;175297&lt;/code&gt; seconds is &lt;strong&gt;2.03 days&lt;/strong&gt;: the sitemap 404 has been cached since the 18th.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And here is the second trap, the one that cost me a correction in the first draft of this piece: &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;age&lt;/code&gt; measures the response, not the deployment.&lt;/strong&gt; A CDN can serve a stale object while the origin already holds the new one; the age of the response does not distinguish "it was never deployed" from "it was deployed and the cache was not refreshed". What these two numbers do prove is the only thing that matters to the reader: the fix does not reach these URLs. Whether the origin has it cannot be known from here — there is no deploy credential and no version identifier in the headers. That field is &lt;code&gt;NO_MEDIDO&lt;/code&gt; (not measured).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three questions, three instruments, and none of them substitutes for another:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;what you want to know&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;what measures it&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;verdict here&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;does the code compile and generate the routes?&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;next build&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;green, RC 0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;does the fix reach these URLs?&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;the response &lt;code&gt;age&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;no&lt;/strong&gt;: 9.06 days old&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;does the origin hold the deployment?&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;deploy credential or version id&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;NO_MEDIDO&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A green local build is not a public act: it is half the act. And a stale response is not proof of a missing deployment: it is proof of a stale response. The two confusions look alike because both feel like a verdict.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The outcome, measured today
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wrote the previous section without a deploy credential. Hours later there were two deployments made from the repository, and I ran the same census again over the same fifteen routes. The result is not the one that section leads you to expect:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;measurement&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;before&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;after&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;routes measured&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;15&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;15&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;distinct titles&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;15&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;repeated titles&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;14&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;routes with &lt;code&gt;rel="canonical"&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;15&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;/sitemap.xml&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;404&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;200, 11 URLs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;/robots.txt&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;Sitemap:&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;no&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;yes&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix covered the eleven URLs declared in the sitemap. The four remaining — &lt;code&gt;/privacy&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;/terms&lt;/code&gt; and the two OAuth authorisation screens — still served the root layout title: they are client components with no server layout, which is literally the blind spot the previous section describes. Four more server layouts and the census closes at 15 of 15.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And a correction to my own plan: I had the Cloudflare purge as a mandatory step before probing. It was not needed. &lt;code&gt;cf-cache-status&lt;/code&gt; answers &lt;code&gt;DYNAMIC&lt;/code&gt; on &lt;code&gt;/&lt;/code&gt; and on &lt;code&gt;/sitemap.xml&lt;/code&gt; — served from the origin — and &lt;code&gt;REVALIDATED&lt;/code&gt; on &lt;code&gt;/robots.txt&lt;/code&gt;, which did have a stored copy and revalidated it against the origin. Which cache produced the old &lt;code&gt;age&lt;/code&gt; is something none of those headers state: that field stays &lt;code&gt;NO_MEDIDO&lt;/code&gt;. What is observed is the only thing that mattered for the act: with the new deployment, none of the three routes serves the previous object any more, and without a manual purge.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What this piece does not claim
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It does not claim&lt;/strong&gt; that fixing the titles will raise traffic. The domain's analytics are not reachable from where this measurement was taken: that number is &lt;code&gt;NO_MEDIDO&lt;/code&gt; (not measured), and &lt;code&gt;NO_MEDIDO&lt;/code&gt; does not read as zero.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It does not claim&lt;/strong&gt; that the patch caused the sitemap 404. It is 2.03 days old and the patch was written today: the 404 predates it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It does not claim&lt;/strong&gt; that the deployment is absent from the origin. It measures that it does not reach the reader; the state of the origin is &lt;code&gt;NO_MEDIDO&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It does not claim&lt;/strong&gt; that the deployment will resolve itself. Between the repository and the domain there is a CDN that can rewrite &lt;code&gt;robots.txt&lt;/code&gt; without consulting the origin; if it does, the &lt;code&gt;Sitemap:&lt;/code&gt; directive from &lt;code&gt;app/robots.ts&lt;/code&gt; will never appear in the response. That is not measured.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It does not claim&lt;/strong&gt; that &lt;code&gt;"use client"&lt;/code&gt; is a mistake. It is a correct directive whose side effect — excluding &lt;code&gt;metadata&lt;/code&gt; — becomes expensive when applied to fourteen routes that need no interactivity.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Reproduce
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The title each dynamic URL serves, in one line:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; s &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; arquitecto-cuantico codigo-origen corazon-punto-cero experiencias-de-lectura \
mente-eterna sala-general sincronicidades suenos-y-meditaciones&lt;span class="o"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;do&lt;/span&gt;
curl -sL &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://arquitectocuantico.com/comunidad/$s"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; grep -o &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'&amp;lt;title&amp;gt;[^&amp;lt;]*&amp;lt;/title&amp;gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;done&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; sort &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; uniq -c
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The edge, in another:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;curl -s -o /dev/null -w &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'%{http_code} age=%header{age}\n'&lt;/span&gt; https://arquitectocuantico.com/sitemap.xml
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;And the count behind the cause, over the repository:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;find src/app -name page.tsx &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; wc -l
grep -rl &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'^"use client"'&lt;/span&gt; src/app --include=page.tsx &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; wc -l
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Single CTA
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before reading the content of any site you inherit, run this (&lt;code&gt;EL-DOMINIO&lt;/code&gt; stands for the domain):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;curl -sL https://EL-DOMINIO/ &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; grep -o &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'&amp;lt;title&amp;gt;[^&amp;lt;]*&amp;lt;/title&amp;gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If that title is also the title of the inner pages, the problem is not in the text: it is in where the declaration lives. And fixing the text does not touch it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The instrument this is measured with: &lt;a href="https://alexisromtz-lab.github.io/auditor-toolkit/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://alexisromtz-lab.github.io/auditor-toolkit/&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Other published measurements: &lt;a href="https://alexisromtz-lab.github.io/la-auditora/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://alexisromtz-lab.github.io/la-auditora/&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://arquitectocuantico.com/?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=articulo&amp;amp;utm_campaign=muro-use-client" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The site this was applied to, measured after deploying&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most "AI trading bot" posts show a chart that goes up and to the right. This is not one of those.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Over the last few months I built a full machine-learning research and signal platform for the Colombo Stock Exchange (CSE) in Sri Lanka, as a solo personal project. It collects the data, cleans it, trains and tests models, and turns the result into clear daily guidance in a web dashboard. It does not trade by itself: every order is still placed by a person.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The biggest lesson? Building the model took maybe 20% of the time. Data, honest testing and guardrails took the rest. Here is how it is put together and what I learned.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Why a small market is hard (and interesting)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The CSE is not the NYSE. A few things make it tricky:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Thin liquidity. Many listed shares trade only a few times a day. A model that loves an illiquid stock is useless if you cannot actually buy it.&lt;br&gt;
High costs. Broker fees are around 1.1% per side, so a round trip costs more than 2%. Any edge has to beat that before it is worth anything.&lt;br&gt;
Messy history. Splits, bonus issues and rights issues show up as huge fake price jumps unless you deal with them.&lt;br&gt;
No clean free dataset. I had to collect it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That shaped every design decision.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The data pipeline&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first job was getting 15+ years of daily prices for 700+ securities: more than 1 million rows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A Python collector with Playwright pulls the history in date windows, retries failures, and resumes where it stopped.&lt;br&gt;
During market hours it records live intraday snapshots every couple of minutes.&lt;br&gt;
It also pulls announcements and daily market-wide flow data from public sources.&lt;br&gt;
Everything lands in SQLite (fast, zero setup) and is mirrored to SQL Server.&lt;br&gt;
Corporate actions: the silent model killer&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A 2-for-1 split looks like a 50% crash overnight. Feed that to a model and it learns nonsense.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So the pipeline flags every suspicious overnight jump and classifies it: a clean split ratio whose new price level persists is adjusted, a move that reverts is kept as a real move, and anything unclear is masked out of training instead of guessed. Unknown is safer than wrong.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The model, and how I tried not to fool myself&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The model itself is plain: gradient-boosted trees (scikit-learn) on price, volume and market-context features. The interesting part is the testing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Walk-forward testing. The model is trained on the past, tested on the next block of time it has never seen, then the window moves forward. Over 15 years that gives 60 out-of-sample blocks. Every prediction in the results was made before the model "knew" what happened.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Real costs in every test. Broker fees on both sides, plus slippage of at least half a price tick. I also found a bug here during QA: I had used one tick size for every share, but the exchange uses a larger tick above a certain price. Small detail, real money.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No look-ahead. A signal at today's close can only be acted on at the next day's open. Easy to get wrong, and it inflates results a lot when you do.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Saying "no edge" out loud. The dashboard has an honest verdict for every configuration, and most variations I tried did not survive. I spent a full day testing new features (sector groups, a "beat the market" target, a liquid-only universe, monthly rebalancing). Several of them improved the overall ranking metrics, but none improved the stocks the system would actually pick, so none went live. That was the most useful day of the project.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Automation with a safety gate&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The system runs unattended on a laptop:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;starts with Windows and resumes collection on its own,&lt;br&gt;
retrains weekly, but the new model only replaces the old one if its out-of-sample score is not worse, the export matches the trained model exactly, and nothing looks off. The old model is backed up first,&lt;br&gt;
paper-trades every signal with the same rules, so there is a live track record before any real money,&lt;br&gt;
backs up the database to the cloud daily,&lt;br&gt;
sends phone and desktop alerts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The safety gate matters. An automatic retrain that silently ships a worse model is worse than no retraining at all.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The dashboard&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The front end is React 19 + TypeScript + Tailwind, served by a tiny Python HTTP server with live updates.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It has 18 pages: a control center, data quality, a model lab, a backtest studio where you can change settings and re-run instantly, paper trading with a journal, risk and system health, news, and a live intraday view.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The page I use most is Today's plan: given how much I want to invest, it shows what to buy (or, most days, "no buy today, and here is why"), what I hold, my break-even price after fees, and when each holding can be sold without a loss.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is also a small browser extension that fills the broker's order form with the system's numbers, because typing quantities by hand is where mistakes happen. It never presses Buy or Sell; that stays a human decision.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Testing the whole thing&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before calling it done I ran a full QA pass:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;unit tests for the money logic (sizing, fees, tick sizes, sell rules, holidays),&lt;br&gt;
integration tests that throw garbage and hostile input at every API endpoint,&lt;br&gt;
UI tests that open every page at desktop and phone width and check for errors,&lt;br&gt;
acceptance tests for the real user flows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That pass found real bugs: a sizing fallback that could suggest a much larger order than the budget, a crash on a bad journal date, and a local API that another website could have called. All fixed, all covered by tests now.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What I would tell someone starting a project like this&lt;br&gt;
Spend your time on data. Your model can only be as honest as your history.&lt;br&gt;
Put real costs in from day one. Many "edges" disappear the moment you add fees.&lt;br&gt;
Test walk-forward, never on data the model has seen.&lt;br&gt;
Make "no" an easy answer. Build the system so it can tell you an idea does not work.&lt;br&gt;
Automate, but with a gate. Retraining without a quality check is a risk, not a feature.&lt;br&gt;
Stack&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Python · scikit-learn · pandas · Playwright · SQLite · SQL Server · React 19 · TypeScript · Tailwind CSS&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The source stays private, but there is a project showcase here: github.com/jkchinthaka/cse-signal-bot&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I build data pipelines, ML systems, dashboards and full-stack web apps as a freelancer. If you are working on something similar, I would be happy to talk.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a research and learning project. Nothing here is investment advice.&lt;br&gt;
(&lt;a href="https://dev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/uploads/articles/mun7hwn9hithtqmhw0rm.png" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://dev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/uploads/articles/mun7hwn9hithtqmhw0rm.png&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Flask and React are a great pairing: fast to build, flexible, and easy to deploy. But that same speed means security often gets pushed to "later," and later usually means after something breaks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I work as a secure software engineer, and these five mistakes keep showing up in tutorials, side projects, and early-stage apps. None of them are exotic. All of them are fixable in a few lines.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Trusting user input without validation&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The mistake: Reading values straight from request.get_json() and using them as if they were safe and correctly shaped.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;python&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ❌ Before&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;route&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/register"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;methods&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"POST"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;register&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get_json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"email"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;          &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# KeyError if missing, anything if present&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;password&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"password"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# no length or type checks&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;create_user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;password&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"message"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"ok"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;201&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Missing fields crash the endpoint, and unexpected types or oversized strings flow deeper into your app.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix: Validate every request body against a schema before touching it. Pydantic works well for this (pip install pydantic email-validator).&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;python&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ✅ After&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;flask&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;jsonify&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pydantic&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;BaseModel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;EmailStr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Field&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ValidationError&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;RegisterSchema&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;BaseModel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;EmailStr&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;password&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Field&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;min_length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;max_length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;128&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Field&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;min_length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;max_length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;80&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;route&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/register"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;methods&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"POST"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;register&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;RegisterSchema&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;**&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get_json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;silent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;True&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;or&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;except&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ValidationError&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;jsonify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"errors"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;errors&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;400&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;create_user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;password&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"message"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"ok"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;201&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Frontend validation in React is good for user experience, but it is not security. Anyone can send requests to your API without your UI. Always validate on the server.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Storing JWTs in localStorage&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The mistake: Saving the access token where any JavaScript on the page can read it.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;javascript&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ❌ Before&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;axios&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/api/login"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;credentials&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;localStorage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setItem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"token"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;token&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;axios&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;defaults&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;common&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Authorization"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`Bearer ${data.token}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If an attacker lands a single XSS payload (a vulnerable dependency, an unsanitized dangerouslySetInnerHTML), they can read localStorage and steal the session.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix: Keep the token in an HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite cookie so JavaScript cannot read it. With flask-jwt-extended:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;python&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ✅ After (Flask)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;flask&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;jsonify&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;flask_jwt_extended&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;JWTManager&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;create_access_token&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;set_access_cookies&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;config&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"JWT_TOKEN_LOCATION"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"cookies"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;config&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"JWT_COOKIE_SECURE"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;True&lt;/span&gt;        &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# HTTPS only&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;config&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"JWT_COOKIE_SAMESITE"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Lax"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;config&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"JWT_COOKIE_CSRF_PROTECT"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;True&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# keep this on with cookies&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;jwt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;JWTManager&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;route&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/login"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;methods&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"POST"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;login&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ...verify credentials...&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;token&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;create_access_token&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;identity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;jsonify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"message"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"logged in"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;set_access_cookies&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;token&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;javascript&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;//&lt;/span&gt; ✅ &lt;span class="nc"&gt;After&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;//&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Send&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cookies&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;with&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;every&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;The&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;token&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;itself&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;never&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;touches&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;JS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;axios&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;defaults&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;withCredentials&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Trade-off to know: cookies are automatically attached by the browser, which opens the door to CSRF. That's why JWT_COOKIE_CSRF_PROTECT stays on, and your React app sends the CSRF token (from the csrf_access_token cookie) in an X-CSRF-TOKEN header on state-changing requests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. A wide-open CORS configuration&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The mistake: Making CORS errors disappear during development with CORS(app) and never tightening it.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;python&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ❌ Before&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;flask_cors&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;CORS&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="no"&gt;CORS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# allows requests from any origin&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Combined with cookie-based auth, an over-permissive policy lets other websites make requests to your API from a visitor's browser.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix: Allow only the origins you actually use, and be explicit about credentials.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;python&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ✅ After&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;flask_cors&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;CORS&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="no"&gt;ALLOWED_ORIGINS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;environ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"ALLOWED_ORIGINS"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"http://localhost:5173"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;split&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;","&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="no"&gt;CORS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;origins&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;ALLOWED_ORIGINS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;supports_credentials&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;True&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;methods&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"GET"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"POST"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"PUT"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"DELETE"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;allow_headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Content-Type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"X-CSRF-TOKEN"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Set ALLOWED_ORIGINS to your real production domain in your deployment environment, and never use * together with credentials.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;4. Building SQL queries with string formatting&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The mistake: Putting user input directly into a SQL string.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;python&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ❌ Before&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;cur&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;execute&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '{email}'"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If email is ' OR '1'='1, the query returns every user. With worse payloads, attackers can modify or delete data. SQL injection is decades old and still one of the most damaging bugs around.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix: Use parameterized queries so the database treats input as data, never as SQL.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;python&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ✅ After (psycopg2 with PostgreSQL)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;cur&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;execute&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = %s"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;python&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ✅ After (SQLAlchemy ORM)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;User&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter_by&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;first&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ✅ After (SQLAlchemy raw SQL, still safe with bound parameters)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sqlalchemy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;session&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;execute&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = :email"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"email"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The rule is simple: if user input is ever concatenated or f-string-ed into a query, stop and parameterize it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;5. Leaking secrets through .env files and frontend bundles&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The mistake: Hardcoding secrets, committing .env to Git, or putting secrets in frontend environment variables.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;python&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ❌ Before&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;config&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"SECRET_KEY"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"supersecret123"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;config&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"DATABASE_URL"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"postgresql://admin:MyPassword@db.example.com/app"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;bash
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# ❌ Before: committed to the repo, and exposed to the browser&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nv"&gt;REACT_APP_STRIPE_SECRET_KEY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;sk_live_...
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Two things go wrong here. Anything committed to Git can live in history forever, even after you delete the file. And any variable prefixed with REACT_APP_ (or VITE_ in Vite) is bundled into your frontend code, so every visitor can read it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;python&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ✅ After (Flask)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dotenv&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;load_dotenv&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;load_dotenv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# reads .env locally; in production use real environment variables&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;config&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"SECRET_KEY"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;environ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"SECRET_KEY"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;config&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"DATABASE_URL"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;environ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"DATABASE_URL"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;bash
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# ✅ .gitignore&lt;/span&gt;
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Commit a .env.example with placeholder values so teammates know what to set.&lt;br&gt;
Keep secrets on the backend only. Frontend variables should hold public values, such as an API base URL.&lt;br&gt;
If a secret was ever committed, rotate it. Deleting the file is not enough.&lt;br&gt;
Add a scanner like gitleaks as a pre-commit hook or CI step to catch leaks early.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When I started learning React, I came across different hooks like &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;useRef&lt;/code&gt;. One hook that I wanted to understand better was &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At first, I wondered:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Why do we need &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt;?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What problem does it solve?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;When should we use it?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's understand it step by step.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1. What is useCallback?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt; is a React Hook that memoizes a function.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In simple words, it helps React reuse the same function between renders instead of creating a new function every time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Syntax:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;memoizedFunction&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useCallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Your logic&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;dependencies&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;It takes two arguments:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A function.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A dependency array.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React returns the same function reference as long as its dependencies haven't changed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


2. Why do we need useCallback?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's look at a simple example.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'react'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;App&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;handleClick&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Button clicked"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Increase&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;handleClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Click&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Me&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;App&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Every time the &lt;code&gt;count&lt;/code&gt; state changes, the component re-renders.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;During that render, JavaScript creates a new &lt;code&gt;handleClick&lt;/code&gt; function.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is where &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt; can help.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3. Using useCallback
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's modify our example.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useCallback&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'react'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;App&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;handleClick&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useCallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Button clicked"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Increase&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;handleClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Click&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Me&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;App&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Now React can reuse the same &lt;code&gt;handleClick&lt;/code&gt; function between renders because the dependency array is empty.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


4. Understanding the dependency array
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The dependency array tells React when it should create a new function.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;handleClick&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useCallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Here, &lt;code&gt;count&lt;/code&gt; is a dependency.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If &lt;code&gt;count&lt;/code&gt; doesn't change, React reuses the previous function.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If &lt;code&gt;count&lt;/code&gt; changes, React returns a new function that uses the updated value.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Forms are where React apps quietly get slow. The classic approach stores every keystroke in state, which re-renders the whole form on every character typed. React Hook Form takes a different path: it reads values from uncontrolled inputs, so your component barely re-renders at all.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Getting started takes minutes:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useForm&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'react-hook-form'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;SignupForm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;register&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;handleSubmit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;formState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;errors&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useForm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onSubmit&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;form&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onSubmit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;handleSubmit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;onSubmit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;register&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'email'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;required&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Email is required'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;errors&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;errors&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"password"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;register&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'password'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;required&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;minLength&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;8&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;errors&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;password&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Password&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;must&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;be&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;at&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;least&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;8&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;characters&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"submit"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Sign&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;up&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;form&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;register&lt;/code&gt; function connects each input to the form. Validation rules like &lt;code&gt;required&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;minLength&lt;/code&gt; live right next to the field, and errors appear in &lt;code&gt;formState.errors&lt;/code&gt; without any extra code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This approach has two big wins. First, performance: typing in one field does not re-render the others, which matters a lot in long forms. Second, less code: no &lt;code&gt;value&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;onChange&lt;/code&gt; handler for every single input.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React Hook Form also plays well with validation libraries like Zod and Yup, so you can define one schema and use it on the client and the server. It works with UI libraries too — there are wrappers for controlled components like date pickers and rich text editors.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React Hook Form is for anyone building signup forms, checkout forms, settings pages, or admin panels in React. If your forms have more than three fields, it will save you real code and real re-renders.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fetching data in React used to mean writing the same &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; + &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt; + loading flag in every component. SWR, built by the Vercel team, replaces all of that with one hook — and adds caching, revalidation, and error handling for free.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The name comes from "stale-while-revalidate": SWR shows you the cached data immediately, then quietly fetches fresh data in the background and updates the UI. Your app feels instant even on a slow network.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the whole idea in a few lines:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useSWR&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'swr'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fetcher&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;then&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;UserProfile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;isLoading&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useSWR&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`/api/users/${id}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fetcher&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isLoading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Loading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Something&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;went&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;wrong&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That is it. No manual loading state, no effect cleanup, no duplicate requests. If two components ask for the same URL, SWR makes one request and shares the result. When you switch tabs and come back, it revalidates automatically. When the network drops and returns, it retries.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SWR also gives you &lt;code&gt;mutate&lt;/code&gt; for optimistic updates. After a user edits their profile, you can update the cached data immediately instead of waiting for a refetch:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;mutate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`/api/users/${id}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'New Name'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;SWR is for React developers who fetch data from APIs and are tired of writing the same loading and error logic everywhere. It is small, focused, and works beautifully with REST APIs and tools like Next.js. If your app mostly reads data and shows it, SWR is one of the simplest upgrades you can make.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your product syncs contacts into whatever CRM a customer connects. Your contact has &lt;code&gt;firstName&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;jobTitle&lt;/code&gt; and a nested &lt;code&gt;company.name&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Customer A wants those in Salesforce &lt;code&gt;FirstName&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Title&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;Company&lt;/code&gt;. Customer B keeps job titles in a custom field. Customer C wants Leads, not Contacts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can't hardcode that. Only the customer, looking at their own account, knows where your &lt;code&gt;jobTitle&lt;/code&gt; belongs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Building it yourself means a settings screen, field-listing endpoints per CRM, per-user storage and a hand-written transform. With ByteChef Embedded it's three pieces:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A &lt;code&gt;field_mapping&lt;/code&gt; input on the integration's workflow&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A Field Mapping step that applies the saved mapping&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A &lt;code&gt;mapObjectFields&lt;/code&gt; config on the Connect dialog that loads options from the user's own account&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is part 14 of the ByteChef Embedded series. The full version, with every edge case and API call, is on the &lt;a href="https://blog.bytechef.io/blogs/embedded-field-mapping" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ByteChef blog&lt;/a&gt;. You need an &lt;code&gt;@bytechef/embedded&lt;/code&gt; release newer than 0.4.0.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Two halves, one string
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Design time:&lt;/strong&gt; you declare a &lt;code&gt;field_mapping&lt;/code&gt; input with &lt;code&gt;"objectName": "Contacts"&lt;/code&gt; and add a Field Mapping step.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Runtime:&lt;/strong&gt; the connected user opens the Connect dialog, picks an object type and pairs each of your fields with one of theirs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The two halves meet on the object name. The workflow stores neither the option lists nor the mapping. Object types and fields come from the user's live connection through callbacks you write, and the Field Mapping step fetches the saved mapping by object name when it runs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The running example
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your product emits a &lt;code&gt;contact.created&lt;/code&gt; app event:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"contactId"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"c_123"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"firstName"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Ada"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"lastName"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Lovelace"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"email"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"ada@example.com"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"jobTitle"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"CTO"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"company"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"name"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Analytical Engines"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;You want a Salesforce workflow, &lt;em&gt;Sync new contacts to Salesforce&lt;/em&gt;, where each customer decides Lead or Contact, and which Salesforce field each of yours lands in.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 1: Declare the input
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Workflow Inputs panel only offers scalar types, so add this one in the Workflow Code Editor (the code icon in the integration workflow's right sidebar) and click Save:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"inputs"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"name"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"contactMapping"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"label"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Contact Mapping"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"field_mapping"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"required"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"objectName"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Contacts"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;type: field_mapping&lt;/code&gt; makes the Connect dialog render the mapping UI instead of a text box.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;objectName&lt;/code&gt; joins the two halves. The match is exact: &lt;code&gt;Contacts&lt;/code&gt; isn't &lt;code&gt;contacts&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;name&lt;/code&gt; is how you read the saved value: &lt;code&gt;${contactMapping}&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;label&lt;/code&gt; is the heading above the mapping in the dialog.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The editor's validation list may say &lt;code&gt;type&lt;/code&gt; must be a scalar type. Ignore it. Saving and publishing still work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 2: Apply it with the Field Mapping component
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Field Mapping lives under &lt;strong&gt;Helpers&lt;/strong&gt; and has one action per direction:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Action&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Turns&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Into&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Use it&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;mapToIntegration&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;your object (&lt;code&gt;firstName&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;jobTitle&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;their shape (&lt;code&gt;FirstName&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Title&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;before writing into the user's app&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;mapToApplication&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;their record (&lt;code&gt;FirstName&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Title&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;your shape (&lt;code&gt;firstName&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;jobTitle&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;before handing a record back to yours&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both take &lt;code&gt;objectName&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;inputType&lt;/code&gt; (&lt;code&gt;OBJECT&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;ARRAY&lt;/code&gt;), &lt;code&gt;data&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;includeUnmapped&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Notice what's missing: the mapping. You never wire the input into the action. At run time it finds the workflow's &lt;code&gt;field_mapping&lt;/code&gt; input by object name and loads the connected user's saved value on its own.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's the complete workflow, ready to paste into the code editor:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"label"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Sync new contacts to Salesforce"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"description"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Creates a Salesforce record for every contact.created event, using the connected user's field mapping."&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"inputs"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"name"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"contactMapping"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"label"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Contact Mapping"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"field_mapping"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"required"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"objectName"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Contacts"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"triggers"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"label"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Contact Created"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"name"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"trigger_1"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"appEvent/v1/newEvent"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"parameters"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"appEventId"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"tasks"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"label"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Map to Salesforce fields"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"name"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"fieldMapping_1"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"fieldMapping/v1/mapToIntegration"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"parameters"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"objectName"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Contacts"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"inputType"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"OBJECT"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"data"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"${trigger_1}"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"includeUnmapped"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"label"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Create Salesforce record"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"name"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"salesforce_1"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"salesforce/v1/createRecord"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"parameters"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"object"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"${contactMapping.objectType}"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"fields"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"${fieldMapping_1}"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Replace &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt; with your own app event's id. For a user who mapped your fields to Lead fields, the mapping step outputs:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"FirstName"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Ada"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"LastName"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Lovelace"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"Email"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"ada@example.com"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"Title"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"CTO"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"Company"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Analytical Engines"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The Salesforce object comes from the mapping too. &lt;code&gt;${contactMapping.objectType}&lt;/code&gt; is the object type the user picked. Rule of thumb: use the data pill when a step needs to &lt;strong&gt;read&lt;/strong&gt; the mapping, and the Field Mapping actions when it needs to &lt;strong&gt;apply&lt;/strong&gt; it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The renaming rules decide what reaches the user's account, so know them:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Dotted paths work both ways.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;company.name&lt;/code&gt; is read out of a nested object, and mapping back builds it again.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Absent keys are skipped.&lt;/strong&gt; A payload without &lt;code&gt;jobTitle&lt;/code&gt; never blanks &lt;code&gt;Title&lt;/code&gt;. A key present with &lt;code&gt;null&lt;/code&gt; copies the null.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Unmapped keys are dropped by default.&lt;/strong&gt; With &lt;code&gt;includeUnmapped&lt;/code&gt; on they pass through under their original names, which a strict API like Salesforce rejects.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Arrays are mapped item by item&lt;/strong&gt;, and every item must be an object.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both actions work from a Script step too, through &lt;code&gt;context.component&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;perform&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;contacts&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;component&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;fieldMapping&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;mapToApplication&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;objectName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Contacts"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;inputType&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"ARRAY"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;records&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;contacts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;contact&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;contact&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Step 3: Give the editor a test value
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The editor's Test button runs without a connected user, so there's no saved mapping to read. Instead the actions look for a &lt;code&gt;sampleMapping&lt;/code&gt; under the object name in the input's test value:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"objectType"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Lead"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"Contacts"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"sampleMapping"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"mappings"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"applicationField"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"custom"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"label"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"First name"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"value"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"firstName"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"integrationField"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"FirstName"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"applicationField"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"custom"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"label"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Last name"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"value"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"lastName"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"integrationField"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"LastName"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"applicationField"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"custom"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"label"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Email"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"value"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"email"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"integrationField"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Email"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"applicationField"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"custom"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"label"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Job title"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"value"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"jobTitle"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"integrationField"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Title"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"applicationField"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"custom"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"label"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Company"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"value"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"company.name"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"integrationField"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Company"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"objectType"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Lead"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Open &lt;strong&gt;Workflow Inputs&lt;/strong&gt;, click the edit icon on &lt;code&gt;contactMapping&lt;/code&gt;, paste it into &lt;strong&gt;Test Value&lt;/strong&gt; and save. Leave the Type list alone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;sampleMapping&lt;/code&gt; has exactly the shape the Connect dialog saves, so you can paste a real one in later. The top-level &lt;code&gt;objectType&lt;/code&gt; makes &lt;code&gt;${contactMapping.objectType}&lt;/code&gt; resolve to &lt;code&gt;Lead&lt;/code&gt; in test runs, so the Create Salesforce record step runs from the editor too. Without a sample, the step fails with a message that tells you to add one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 4: Publish and enable
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Publish the integration.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Make sure its configuration is enabled, on the new version, with this workflow enabled in it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The user connects Salesforce in the Connect dialog and enables the workflow. The mapping shows up among that workflow's inputs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 5: Wire the Connect dialog
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Pass &lt;code&gt;mapObjectFields&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;useConnectDialog&lt;/code&gt;, keyed by the same object name:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;useConnectDialog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;MapObjectFieldsType&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;OptionType&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@bytechef/embedded'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;SoqlResponseI&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;records&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Array&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;QualifiedApiName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;SALESFORCE_OBJECT_TYPES&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;OptionType&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Lead'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Lead'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Contact'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Contact'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;mapObjectFields&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;MapObjectFieldsType&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Contacts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;objectTypes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;SALESFORCE_OBJECT_TYPES&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;integrationFields&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;executeAction&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;objectType&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;executeAction&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'salesforce'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'soqlQuery'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`SELECT QualifiedApiName, Label FROM FieldDefinition WHERE EntityDefinition.QualifiedApiName = '${objectType}'`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;SoqlResponseI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;records&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;record&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;OptionType&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;record&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;record&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;QualifiedApiName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sort&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;first&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;second&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;first&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;localeCompare&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;second&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;applicationFields&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;fields&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'First name'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'firstName'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Last name'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'lastName'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Email'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'email'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Job title'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'jobTitle'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Company'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'company.name'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;userCanCreateFields&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;userCanRemoveMappings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ConnectSalesforceButton&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;integrationId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;jwtToken&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;integrationId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;jwtToken&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;openDialog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useConnectDialog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;baseUrl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'https://bytechef.example.com'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;environment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'DEVELOPMENT'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;integrationId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;jwtToken&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;mapObjectFields&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;openDialog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Connect&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Salesforce&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Each entry has three parts:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;objectTypes.get&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; fills the Object type select. It doesn't have to call the remote system. A fixed list guarantees the &lt;code&gt;objectType&lt;/code&gt; the next callback receives is one you chose.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;integrationFields.get&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; runs each time the user picks an object type. Here it runs Salesforce's SOQL Query action on the user's own connection against &lt;code&gt;FieldDefinition&lt;/code&gt;, so custom fields show up too.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;applicationFields&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; is your side of the table: &lt;code&gt;fields&lt;/code&gt; (values can be dotted paths like &lt;code&gt;company.name&lt;/code&gt;), optional &lt;code&gt;defaultFields&lt;/code&gt;, plus &lt;code&gt;userCanRemoveMappings&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;userCanCreateFields&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Using the Integration Marketplace instead of your own page? Pass the same object to its &lt;code&gt;mapObjectFields&lt;/code&gt; prop.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


What &lt;code&gt;executeAction&lt;/code&gt; actually does
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You never build it. The SDK creates it, bound to the current user and the integration instance being configured. Each call is one request to the same embedded actions endpoint the ComponentKit uses, with the user's JWT and an &lt;code&gt;X-Instance-Id&lt;/code&gt; header. ByteChef checks that the JWT's user owns that instance and answers 403 if not, so a callback can't reach another user's account.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two things that will bite you:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It resolves to whatever the action returns. It's typed as an array, but SOQL Query returns an object, hence the cast.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It never throws. A network error or error status is logged to the console as &lt;code&gt;Failed to execute action&lt;/code&gt; and resolves to &lt;code&gt;[]&lt;/code&gt;. An unexpectedly empty select means: check the console.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What the user sees
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The mapping sits with every other workflow input in the Connect dialog's managed view. Here's that view for a workflow with no inputs:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fqdrgoppuzaby3b285e71.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fqdrgoppuzaby3b285e71.png" alt="Connect dialog for a connected integration, with a Workflows tab, one enabled workflow and an INPUTS section that reads No inputs defined for this workflow" width="520" height="420"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With the example workflow enabled, the INPUTS section holds a &lt;strong&gt;Contact Mapping&lt;/strong&gt; heading, an &lt;strong&gt;Object type&lt;/strong&gt; select, and one row per application field. Each row's select stays disabled until an object type is chosen, then lists what &lt;code&gt;integrationFields.get&lt;/code&gt; returned.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There's no Save button. Every change saves on its own, 600 milliseconds after the user stops editing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The mapping is stored as the input's value. Your app can read it back with &lt;code&gt;GET /api/embedded/v1/integrations/{id}&lt;/code&gt; to show something like "Leads, 5 fields mapped" in your own UI. Your backend can pre-fill a sensible default with your environment's API secret key right after a user connects. Send all of the workflow's inputs when you do: whatever you leave out is removed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Try it in the sample app
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The embedded sample app ships a &lt;strong&gt;Field Mapping Playground&lt;/strong&gt; at &lt;code&gt;/field-mapping&lt;/code&gt;. It walks through preparing the workflow, describing your application's fields, mapping in the Connect dialog, reading the stored mapping back and previewing a sample record in both directions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fyouv0sbp2uce2vsv0d3b.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fyouv0sbp2uce2vsv0d3b.png" alt="The Field Mapping Playground: the Describe Your Application Fields card with Object Name Contacts and rows for firstName, lastName, email, jobTitle and company.name, next to the Map in the Connect Dialog card" width="800" height="500"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F6j65hknj28602q6cxrgc.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F6j65hknj28602q6cxrgc.png" alt="The Preview the Mapping card with the example mapping and a sample contact, next to the Result card showing mapToIntegration output and the round trip back through mapToApplication" width="800" height="500"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One warning: the sample's Integrations pages call &lt;code&gt;listObjects&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;listObjectFields&lt;/code&gt; on the &lt;code&gt;salesforce&lt;/code&gt; component as placeholders. Those actions don't exist, so the selects stay empty. Swap in the SOQL Query callback above.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Gotchas
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Text box instead of a mapping editor?&lt;/strong&gt; You're on &lt;code&gt;@bytechef/embedded&lt;/code&gt; 0.4.0. Upgrade before you debug your callbacks.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No &lt;code&gt;mapObjectFields&lt;/code&gt; entry for the object name&lt;/strong&gt; means the dialog renders nothing for that input. No error, no placeholder.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Two &lt;code&gt;field_mapping&lt;/code&gt; inputs with the same object name&lt;/strong&gt; make the Field Mapping actions fail rather than guess.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Switching Lead to Contact keeps existing pairings&lt;/strong&gt;, which may name fields the new object lacks. Have users review every row.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Rows come from your config every time.&lt;/strong&gt; A custom field added earlier stays in the saved value but gets no row back, and the next change saves without it. If custom fields matter, list the common ones in &lt;code&gt;fields&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;required&lt;/code&gt; isn't enforced in the dialog.&lt;/strong&gt; The user can close it with nothing mapped. The example uses &lt;code&gt;required: false&lt;/code&gt; so the failure shows up as a readable Field Mapping error in Workflow Executions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The mapping can't know the target's rules.&lt;/strong&gt; Salesforce won't create a Lead without &lt;code&gt;LastName&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;Company&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Build queries from values you trust.&lt;/strong&gt; The SOQL string is only safe because &lt;code&gt;objectTypes.get&lt;/code&gt; returns a fixed list. Validate the value if object types come from the remote system.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Before you ship
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run an &lt;code&gt;@bytechef/embedded&lt;/code&gt; release newer than 0.4.0 in every frontend that shows the Connect dialog.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Replace placeholder callbacks with actions your integration really has. Try them in the ComponentKit first.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pass &lt;code&gt;environment&lt;/code&gt; explicitly. It defaults to &lt;code&gt;PRODUCTION&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;executeAction&lt;/code&gt; uses it too.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Write a &lt;code&gt;sampleMapping&lt;/code&gt; for every field-mapping input.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pre-fill defaults from your backend and point users at the mapping right after they connect.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Test the full loop per integration: connect, map, trigger an event, check the run and the record in the user's app.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Who owns what
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;You own:&lt;/strong&gt; the field-mapping input and its object name, the workflow and its test value, which of your fields are mappable, the callbacks that decide which remote objects and fields are offered, and any defaults your backend writes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;ByteChef owns:&lt;/strong&gt; the mapping UI, running your callbacks' actions on the user's credentials with ownership checks, storing every user's mapping, null-safe nested renaming in both directions, and the run-time lookup by object name.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One input, one step, one config object with two callbacks. Every customer gets a mapping screen for their own account.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;Next in the series: &lt;strong&gt;Shared Connections&lt;/strong&gt;, for when the account is yours instead of the user's, like the Slack workspace your alerts go to or the API key you pay for.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ByteChef is an open-source workflow automation and AI agent platform (Apache 2.0). Star it on &lt;a href="https://github.com/bytechefhq/bytechef" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt; and read the full guide on the &lt;a href="https://blog.bytechef.io/blogs/embedded-field-mapping" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ByteChef blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The best scientific charting libraries in 2026 are SciChart, Chart.js, Plotly, VTK and D3.js, but for laboratory instrumentation software the choice is a platform decision, not a widget choice. Instrument makers rarely ship a single application. They ship a suite: acquisition and control software at the instrument, analysis tools on the workstation, and increasingly a browser view so colleagues can review results from another lab, or another country.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The chart library underneath sets how every one of those products looks, behaves and gets validated for years. A scientist analyzing a chromatogram, an IR spectrum or a mass spectrum should be able to zoom, compare and annotate the same way in every tool you ship.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This scientific charting library comparison covers the five scientific data visualization libraries that research labs and instrument teams shortlist most in 2026: SciChart, Chart.js, Plotly, VTK and D3.js. It is written for the product owners and project managers who write the requirements, as well as the developers who build them. We built SciChart, so for every other library we have relied on its own documentation rather than our opinion of it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


Table of Contents:
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What Are the Best Scientific Charting Libraries for JavaScript and WPF in 2026?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What Do Scientific Instrument Teams Actually Need From a Charting Library?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Which Charting Library Handles Large Scientific Datasets With Annotations and Overlays?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;How Do You Choose a Cross-Platform Charting Library for JavaScript, WPF, and Mobile Lab Software?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Which Chart Types Do Chromatography, Spectroscopy and Mass Spectrometry Software Need?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Why Does Supportability Matter for Regulated Lab and Diagnostic Software?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What Should Product Owners Put in a Charting Library Requirements Spec?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What Should Developers Check When Evaluating a Scientific Charting Library?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Which Instrument Makers and Research Teams Use SciChart?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Key Takeaways
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;For instrument software, consistency across a product suite matters as much as speed. Labs standardize on software platforms to cut training and validation effort, and the charts inside those platforms need to behave the same everywhere.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Performance is the entry ticket, not the deciding factor. Plotly's documentation flags SVG slowdowns above roughly 15,000 points. SciChart.js can render up to 1 billion data points in browsers that support 64-bit WebAssembly, and SciChart WPF handles billions per dataset.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Analysis depth decides the shortlist: zooming into peaks, overlaying samples, highlighting bands and annotating results, all on large datasets at the same time.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Many instrument suites pair a desktop application with browser-based review, so JavaScript and WPF support behind one consistent API is a practical requirement.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;In regulated settings, a supported commercial component with versioned releases and documented limitations is easier to qualify than an unsupported one.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;For developers, the practical tests are framework fit, how the library handles many charts and streaming data, and how quickly you can get answers from documentation and support.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No neutral benchmark compares all five libraries. Test each one with your own data, annotations and overlays included.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What Are the Best Scientific Charting Libraries for JavaScript and WPF in 2026?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The strongest options in 2026 are SciChart, Chart.js, Plotly, VTK and D3.js. They differ most in how they draw pixels and where they run, and those two facts decide whether one library can cover an entire instrument suite.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Library&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Rendering Architecture&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Platforms Supported&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Best Fit&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;SciChart&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Visual Xccelerator™ C++ engine: WebGL and WebAssembly in the browser, DirectX on Windows, Metal on iOS and macOS, OpenGL on Android&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;JavaScript (React, Angular, Vue, TypeScript), WPF, iOS and macOS, Android, Avalonia XPF (Windows and Linux)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Instrument suites that need the same charts and interactions on desktop and web, with large datasets and rich annotation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Chart.js&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;HTML Canvas&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Web&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Standard charts and reporting dashboards with modest datasets&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Plotly&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;SVG by default, WebGL for accelerated traces&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Web, plus Python and R tooling&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Exploratory and statistical analysis&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;VTK&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;C++ toolkit; vtk.js renders through WebGL and WebGPU&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Desktop and web&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;3D meshes, volumes and medical imaging&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;D3.js&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;SVG, Canvas and HTML primitives&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Web&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Bespoke visuals built from scratch&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For instrumentation teams that need scientific charting libraries for JavaScript and WPF from one vendor, the platforms column matters most. Chart.js and D3.js live in the browser, so a WPF or native desktop application needs a second chart layer with its own behavior, bugs and look. Plotly suits analysts working in Python and R, but it is not a component you embed in a WPF acquisition app.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The best fit column shows how far each library goes before your team starts building features itself. D3.js gives you drawing primitives rather than finished charts. VTK is the specialist for volumetric rendering such as CT and MRI stacks. SciChart is a GPU-accelerated charting library for JavaScript, React, WPF, iOS and Android, built as charting components for laboratory instrumentation, real-time data and large scientific datasets.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What Do Scientific Instrument Teams Actually Need From a Charting Library?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instrument software teams need three things from a charting library: consistency across their product suite, depth of analysis, and freedom to customize. Performance matters, but only once those three are in place.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Consistency Across an Instrument Suite
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instrument makers typically ship a family of products, and their customers expect that family to feel like one system. The same zoom, the same selection, the same branding, wherever the scientist sits. It works the same way as a family of medical devices: once people learn one, they can use the rest faster and with fewer mistakes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Lab buyers make this explicit when they choose software. When Waters launched its Empower 3 chromatography data system, it named &lt;a href="https://www.biospace.com/waters-corporation-empower-3-answers-call-for-a-standardized-chromatography-data-system-at-enterprise-level" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;reduced training and support effort, plus easier software validation&lt;/a&gt; as the benefits of standardizing on one platform. Agilent makes the same case: a single data system &lt;a href="https://www.agilent.com/about/newsroom/presrel/2020/02jan-ca20001.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;saves time managing software, training staff and meeting regulations&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The pain is real on the bench: scientists can end up &lt;a href="https://unitelabs.io/resources/blog/lab-data-management/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;juggling four vendor software tools to complete a single QC run&lt;/a&gt;. UX research explains why consistency matters: Nielsen Norman Group defines internal consistency as applying &lt;a href="https://www.nngroup.com/articles/consistency-and-standards/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;across a family or suite of applications&lt;/a&gt;, and links it to learnability and reduced confusion. A chart library with one API across JavaScript, WPF and mobile lets an instrument maker deliver that consistency without writing three chart layers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Depth of Analysis
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In most labs the hard part is not the size of the dataset. Plenty of scientific work, such as chromatography or IR spectroscopy, comes from a single detector. The hard part is what scientists need to do with the data once it is on screen:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Zoom into peaks and troughs, isolate them and expand specific regions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Overlay multiple samples and compare them like for like&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Highlight specific bands or regions of interest&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Annotate results, with markers, notes, dates and times&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Color individual points and attach metadata to them&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use logarithmic axes, scientific notation and multiple axes on one chart&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run reports and export results&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instrument makers are building exactly these interactions into their own tools: Waters' browser-based &lt;a href="https://www.technologynetworks.com/informatics/products/empower-data-viewer-412842" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Empower Data Viewer&lt;/a&gt; lets users view chromatograms, zoom into peaks, and overlay and compare data. When software cannot support the analysis, scientists export the data and continue somewhere else. A UX study of scientific instrument software found file export is &lt;a href="https://creative.navy/blog/scientific-instrument-software-ux-failures-ebsd-taxonomy" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;the universal handoff point between tools&lt;/a&gt;, a pattern facilities call the workflow seam. Every feature missing from the chart layer widens that seam.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Freedom to Customize
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Scientists investigate data in their own ways, and instrument makers need their software to look like their brand. A charting library for this market should support:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Full theming and white-labeling, including your own colors, fonts and styles&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Dark and light modes, and palettes users can configure themselves&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Accessible color choices for people who read color differently&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Custom workflows and interactions built on top of the standard chart&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;An extensible API, so you can build new visualization types when a customer needs one&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A choice between automatic resampling for very large datasets and presenting the raw data exactly as acquired, since some customers have strict requirements about how data is displayed&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In SciChart, &lt;a href="https://www.scichart.com/documentation/js/current/2d-charts/chart-types/data-series-api/resampling/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;resampling is on by default and designed to be visually lossless&lt;/a&gt;, and it can be switched off per series when data must be shown exactly as acquired, at some cost to performance on very large datasets.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Which Charting Library Handles Large Scientific Datasets With Annotations and Overlays?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;GPU-accelerated libraries such as SciChart handle large scientific datasets with annotations and overlays best, because rendering architecture, not point count alone, decides whether a library survives a real workflow. Every annotation, overlay, extra series, extra axis and piece of metadata adds work to each frame. A library that copes with one clean line can stall once a scientist overlays ten samples and marks up the peaks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The reason is how each approach draws. SVG creates a separate page element for every mark, so the browser slows as marks multiply. Canvas avoids that overhead but still draws on the CPU. GPU engines built on WebGL and WebAssembly hand the drawing to the graphics card, which is why they scale into millions of points and beyond. At 60 FPS, each frame gets about 16.7 milliseconds to take in new data, draw it and respond to the user.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Renderer&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Documented Limit or Guidance&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Source&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Plotly SVG traces&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Slows above roughly 15,000 points&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Plotly Dash documentation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Plotly WebGL traces&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Up to around 1 million points&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Plotly documentation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Plotly WebGL figures per page&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Generally 4 to 8 before hitting browser context limits&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Plotly documentation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Chart.js Canvas&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No published ceiling; decimation recommended for large datasets&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Chart.js documentation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;SciChart.js (JavaScript and React)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Up to 1 billion points with the 64-bit WebAssembly mode (Chrome, Edge and Firefox); about 250 million with the standard build&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;SciChart&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;SciChart WPF&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Billions of points per dataset&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;SciChart&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Figures, ours included, come from each vendor's own documentation and test setups. The Chart.js performance guide suggests decimating data before it reaches the chart and rendering in a web worker with OffscreenCanvas.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Many Charts on One Screen
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Scientific dashboards often show many linked views at once. Plotly's documentation says you will generally fit 4 to 8 WebGL figures on one page before hitting browser context limits. A twelve-panel view needs a workaround such as virtual WebGL, which Plotly notes runs slower. SciChart.js avoids that limit because, by default, it &lt;a href="https://www.scichart.com/documentation/js/current/Performance%20Tips.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;uses a single shared WebGL context for all charts&lt;/a&gt;, and its &lt;a href="https://www.scichart.com/documentation/js/current/2d-charts/subcharts-api/subcharts-api-overview/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;SubCharts API&lt;/a&gt; draws large grids of charts in one pass. SciChart publishes a figure of 64+ charts refreshed in under one second.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Long Sessions and Memory
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instrument software often runs for hours or days. SciChart lists &lt;a href="https://www.scichart.com/why-scichart-diagnostics-lifesciences/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;continuous 24/7 operation and zero memory growth&lt;/a&gt; among its published performance metrics, so long analyses and overnight acquisitions do not degrade. In the browser, SciChart.js holds chart data in WebAssembly memory rather than JavaScript arrays: up to 4 GB in the standard build, and up to 16 GB in 64-bit WebAssembly (wasm64) mode. The same rendering technology now supports &lt;a href="https://www.scichart.com/why-scichart-best-for-motorsport-and-automotive/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;most Formula One telemetry&lt;/a&gt;, simulation and engineering workflows, directly or indirectly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Lightweight Enough for Inexpensive Hardware
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Efficient rendering also lowers hardware requirements. That matters when an instrument ships with an embedded PC, or when field teams use low-cost tablets. PerkinElmer found SciChart's lower hardware requirements kept production costs down across its chromatography product range (see the case studies below).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Performance With Real Features Switched on
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The most useful benchmark is one that turns on the features your workflow needs. SciChart's &lt;a href="https://github.com/abtsoftware/javascript-chart-performance-test-suite" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;open-source JavaScript chart performance test suite&lt;/a&gt; compares libraries across scenarios such as multi-series and multi-axis charts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How Do You Choose a Cross-Platform Charting Library for JavaScript, WPF, and Mobile Lab Software?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with where each product in your suite runs, then pick a cross-platform charting library that covers all of it with the same API. For many instrument makers that means a desktop application at the instrument plus a browser view for review, with mobile for field or portable work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Where it Runs&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Framework&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Typical Lab Use&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Libraries That Fit&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instrument workstation&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;WPF (.NET)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Instrument control, data acquisition, analysis&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;SciChart WPF&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Browser&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;JavaScript, React&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Remote review, shared dashboards, results sign-off&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;SciChart.js, Plotly, Chart.js, D3.js, vtk.js&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Embedded or custom hardware&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Native C++, Android, single-board computer&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Instrument UIs, 3D processing pipelines&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;VTK, SciChart Android, SciChart.js&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Phones and tablets&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;iOS (Swift), Android (Kotlin)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Field analysis, portable instruments&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;SciChart iOS and Android&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Why Desktop Plus Web Is Becoming the Norm
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Modern science rarely happens in one lab. Teams share data across sites and review results from wherever they are. A typical pattern is an installable desktop application at the instrument, with a web view so a colleague can analyze mass spectrometry data from another location.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instrument makers are building for this. Anton Paar's AP Connect lab execution system lets users &lt;a href="https://www.anton-paar.com/corp-en/products/details/ap-connect/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;work with data from any connected client&lt;/a&gt; without returning to the lab bench, with multiple users reviewing data at the same time. Field work follows the same logic: a chromatography analysis run on a mobile device can be uploaded and reviewed elsewhere in exactly the same form.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SciChart supports this with SciChart WPF as the WPF scientific charting library on the desktop, SciChart.js in the browser, native iOS and Android SDKs, and Avalonia XPF for Windows and Linux, all sharing one engine and a consistent API. SciChart.js can also run anywhere a modern browser does, including desktop shells such as Electron, so teams can start with JavaScript everywhere and go native where they need to. For embedded instruments, SciChart Android runs on custom hardware as well as phones and tablets, and &lt;a href="https://www.scichart.com/scichart-js-v5-released/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;SciChart.js v5 was optimized for single-board computers&lt;/a&gt; in industrial and IoT environments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Three Problems With Building Your Own Chart Layer per Platform
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Rendering bottlenecks appear the moment high-frequency or annotation-heavy data outpaces the renderer.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Code duplication is the slower burn. Three platforms means three chart layers, three QA passes and three sets of bugs for one feature, and three slightly different user experiences.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Memory leaks and thermal throttling hit long sessions. A chart that was fine in a ten-minute test can crawl six hours into an experiment, or overheat a tablet at a hot field station. Fixed-size FIFO buffers help, as old points drop off when new ones arrive.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A shared engine removes all three. A fix or feature written once reaches desktop, web and mobile together, and your users get the same interactions, branding and workflows on every screen.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Which Chart Types Do Chromatography, Spectroscopy and Mass Spectrometry Software Need?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each analytical technique has its own standard views, and chromatography and mass spectrometry chart software, like spectroscopy tools, should handle them without custom drawing code. Use this list to check each candidate against the techniques your instruments cover, including the ones on your roadmap.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Technique&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Typical Visualizations&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Chromatography&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Chromatograms for HPLC, GC and LC, peak integration, run overlays, calibration curves&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mass spectrometry&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Mass spectra from GC-MS and LC-MS, TIC and XIC traces, compound comparisons, MS peak integration&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;IR, FTIR, and Raman spectroscopy&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Spectra with peak analysis, overlays, spectrograms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;NMR&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;NMR spectra, overlays, peak picking&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Proteomics&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Peptide plots, spectra, compound comparisons&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;qPCR&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Amplification curves, melt curves&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Flow cytometry&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Scatter plots, density scatter plots, histograms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Particle analysis&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Size distributions, histograms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Thermal analysis&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Temperature sweeps, time-resolved data&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Electrochemistry&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Voltage, charge and discharge curves, impedance plots&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;X-ray diffraction (XRD)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Diffraction profiles&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Kinetics and stability studies&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Time-resolved curves, overlays&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Across these techniques, the building blocks repeat: XY line and scatter charts, logarithmic axes in scientific notation, multiple axes, contour plots, dense heatmaps, spectrograms, 3D surfaces and waterfalls.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;General-purpose libraries leave much of this to you. Chart.js focuses on standard business charts, and D3.js gives you primitives to build from. Plotly and VTK each cover parts of the list. SciChart provides these chart types out of the box, and its API is extensible, so when a customer needs a visualization that does not exist yet, you can build it on the same engine rather than starting over.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why Does Supportability Matter for Regulated Lab and Diagnostic Software?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In regulated settings, the chart library is a component someone has to qualify, so supportability becomes a selection criterion alongside features and speed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Medical device software is the clearest example. Under IEC 62304, a third-party library is treated as SOUP (software of unknown provenance). Manufacturers must &lt;a href="https://meddeviceguide.com/blog/soup-software-unknown-provenance-iec-62304-guide" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;review its known anomalies against the device's intended use&lt;/a&gt; and keep monitoring for new ones. Pharma and analytical labs carry a similar burden: Waters cites &lt;a href="https://www.biospace.com/waters-corporation-empower-3-answers-call-for-a-standardized-chromatography-data-system-at-enterprise-level" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;easier software validation&lt;/a&gt; as a key reason labs standardize on one platform.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That makes some library qualities worth checking before features:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Versioned releases with release notes, so you can pin and track exactly what you ship&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Documented limitations and known issues&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Direct technical support from the people who build the library&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Active, long-term development, with published changelogs for every platform; SciChart aims to answer support tickets within one business day.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As an example of a documented limitation: in SciChart WPF, annotations are not rendered by the GPU engine, so we recommend keeping to around 1,000 annotations per chart and drawing large marker sets as a series instead.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SciChart is trusted in rigorously assessed environments, from cancer diagnostics research with the Mayo Clinic to clinical neurotechnology at NeuroRestore. A commercial component with direct support, versioned releases and documented behavior is easier to qualify than one with no accountable vendor.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What Should Product Owners Put in a Charting Library Requirements Spec?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A good requirements spec tests a charting library against your whole product suite and its next few years, not just today's demo. A 2026 library selection guide warns that teams who treat charting as an isolated front-end choice choose well for the demo but poorly for year three. Cover these points:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Platforms, now and on the roadmap: desktop (WPF), browser (JavaScript or React), mobile and embedded.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;API consistency, so the same interactions and styling work on every platform you ship.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Worst-case data volume, tested with annotations, overlays, multiple series and multiple axes switched on.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Analysis interactions: zoom, pan, peak isolation, sample overlays, band highlighting, cursors and export.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Chart types for every technique your instruments cover, including planned ones.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Theming, branding, dark and light modes, and accessible color palettes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Data display rules: whether raw data must be shown exactly as acquired, or resampling is acceptable.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Long-run stability over a full working day or an overnight acquisition.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Hardware requirements, especially for embedded PCs and low-cost field devices.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Supportability: versioned releases, documented limitations, direct support and long-term development.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Licensing terms for commercial distribution across your product range.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What Should Developers Check When Evaluating a Scientific Charting Library?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers building instruments and lab software should test a charting library in their real stack: how it fits their framework, how it handles streaming data and many charts, and how quickly they can get unstuck. A demo with one clean chart answers none of those questions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Framework and Platform Fit
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Web:&lt;/strong&gt; SciChart.js supports TypeScript, React, Angular and Vue, with WebAssembly and WebGL acceleration. Chart.js, Plotly and D3.js are browser-only.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Windows desktop:&lt;/strong&gt; SciChart WPF supports .NET and .NET Framework applications in C# and VB, rendering on DirectX.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mobile and embedded:&lt;/strong&gt; native iOS and macOS (Swift, Objective-C) and Android (Kotlin, Java) SDKs, including Android on custom hardware.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Linux desktop:&lt;/strong&gt; Avalonia XPF, or SciChart.js in a browser or Electron shell.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Gaps to know about:&lt;/strong&gt; SciChart has no React Native wrapper (use the native SDKs or SciChart.js in a WebView) and no Python library (Plotly or Matplotlib suit notebook analysis).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Data Handling
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Typed data series with Float64 arrays and FIFO mode for continuous streaming&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Automatic, visually lossless resampling, switchable off per series for raw display&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Up to about 250 million XY points with the standard WebAssembly build (4 GB), and up to 1 billion with the optional 64-bit WebAssembly mode (16 GB) in Chrome, Edge and Firefox&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Many Charts on One Page
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Browsers allow only a handful of WebGL contexts per page, which is why Plotly's documentation &lt;a href="https://plotly.com/javascript/webgl-vs-svg/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;caps most pages at 4 to 8 WebGL figures&lt;/a&gt;. SciChart.js shares one WebGL context across all charts by default, and the SubCharts API handles large dashboard grids.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Habits That Keep Any Chart Library Fast
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Keep X values sorted: hit-testing, indexing and resampling all depend on it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Batch updates. Pushing more data less often beats a flood of tiny appends.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Decimate early if your library does not resample. Chart.js advises reducing data before it reaches the chart.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Count your WebGL contexts on multi-chart pages, especially with Plotly.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Profile over a full working day or an overnight run. Memory leaks hide in long sessions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Getting Unstuck
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Check the documentation depth, examples and support model before you commit. SciChart offers &lt;a href="https://www.scichart.com/examples/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;over 350 chart examples&lt;/a&gt;, public forums with thousands of answered questions, an AI assistant trained on its docs, and direct technical support.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Which Instrument Makers and Research Teams Use SciChart?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Four published SciChart stories show what consistency, analysis depth and stability mean in practice, from chromatography and protein analysis to cancer diagnostics and spinal cord injury research.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


PerkinElmer: A Chromatography Platform Built on Usability and Flexibility
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;PerkinElmer's GC series chromatography platform competes in a crowded sector where usability and flexibility are the differentiators. SciChart's lower hardware requirements also kept production costs down across the product range. For an instrument maker, that is the suite argument in one sentence: the same capable charts on every product, without raising the hardware bill. SciChart's customizable charts were used to build PerkinElmer's SimplicityChrom CDS software, where deep configuration and complex annotation overlays let users tailor report outputs to their needs while keeping the software intuitive.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.scichart.com/why-scichart-diagnostics-lifesciences/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Find Out More&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


NanoTemper: Protein Stability Analysis
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;NanoTemper builds instruments for biophysical characterization of proteins, including protein stability analysis. NanoTemper needed to display multi-parameter information on conformational and colloidal stability from its Prometheus device, so users could spot liabilities or stability enhancements that other technologies missed. SciChart was selected to present the high-resolution data with consistent reliability, backed by 64-bit, double-precision charting.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


University of Illinois and Mayo Clinic: Infrared Spectroscopy for Cancer Diagnostics
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fq005fcvsgpnp0nfjr2o2.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fq005fcvsgpnp0nfjr2o2.jpg" alt="close up of scicharts cancer research charting software" width="800" height="450"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The University of Illinois and the Mayo Clinic built the first laser-scanned confocal microscope for infrared spectroscopy, using SciChart heatmaps for real-time imaging. The project helped cut tumor biopsy times from weeks to hours. Dense, constantly refreshing heatmaps are where Canvas and SVG tend to buckle first, so the rendering engine carried real weight.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


NeuroRestore: Helping Paralyzed Patients Walk Again
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;  &lt;iframe src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/4wUADfnCMdc" width="710" height="399"&gt;
&lt;/iframe&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;NeuroRestore is a Swiss treatment center built on research by neuroscientists Grégoire Courtine and Jocelyne Bloch, aiming to help paralyzed patients walk again. The clinical team needed to watch up to 16 EMG sensors at once, alongside the epidural stimulation being delivered. The stimulation hardware updated frequency, amplitude and timing across up to 10 waveforms in real time, and researchers had to see the waveforms and muscle responses together, with no lag, to tune programs on the spot. EPFL reported that participants took up to 300 independent steps on the first day of stimulation. The real-time display was built on SciChart WPF.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.scichart.com/case-studies/paralyzed-patients-walk-again/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Read Case Study&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Which Charting Library Should Your Team Test First?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Match the shortlist to your product suite, your techniques and your data.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Standard web charts with small datasets suit Chart.js.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Bespoke one-off graphics suit D3.js.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Exploratory analysis in Python or R suits Plotly.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;3D volumes and medical imaging suit VTK.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If you are building instrument software that needs consistent, deeply interactive scientific charts across desktop, web and mobile, with large datasets, SciChart belongs on your shortlist.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Rankings of the best data visualization tools for scientific research in 2026 tend to compare feature lists, and demos flatter every library. Test each candidate with data close to what your instruments actually produce, with the overlays, annotations and multiple axes your scientists will use, over a full working day.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The free SciChart.js community license lets you test JavaScript and React performance on real data, for non-commercial use or a commercial evaluation. It carries a watermark, which a paid license removes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Try SciChart with your own data, explore our scientific chart examples, or browse the &lt;a href="https://www.scichart.com/demo/react" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;React chart demos&lt;/a&gt; to see GPU rendering running live in the browser. If you would like help matching SciChart to your platforms and instruments, our team is happy to talk it through.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


FAQs
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


What is the best charting library for scientific instrument software?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It depends on your platforms and data. For suites that need the same interactive charts on desktop, web and mobile, with large datasets and rich annotation, SciChart is built for the job. For browser-only reporting, Chart.js is a simpler option.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Does SciChart support JavaScript and WPF?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. SciChart offers SciChart.js for JavaScript and React, SciChart WPF for Windows desktop applications, and native SDKs for iOS and Android, all built on the same engine.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Can SciChart be used for chromatography software?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. SciChart supports HPLC and GC chromatograms, run overlays, peak integration views and calibration curves, and is used in PerkinElmer's GC chromatography platform.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Can SciChart be used for mass spectrometry?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. SciChart handles mass spectra, TIC and XIC traces and compound comparisons, with zooming, overlays and annotations for peak analysis.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Can SciChart display IR, FTIR, Raman and NMR spectra?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. Spectra, overlays, logarithmic axes in scientific notation and spectrograms are supported. SciChart heatmaps powered real-time infrared spectroscopy imaging at the University of Illinois and the Mayo Clinic.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Can SciChart plot qPCR amplification and melt curves?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. Amplification and melt curves are XY line charts with multiple series, overlays and annotations, all supported out of the box.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Can SciChart be used for flow cytometry and particle analysis?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. SciChart supports scatter and density scatter plots, histograms and size distributions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Where can SciChart be used in scientific and lab software?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SciChart is used across analytical and life sciences instrumentation, including chromatography (HPLC, GC, LC), mass spectrometry (GC-MS, LC-MS), IR, FTIR, Raman and NMR spectroscopy, proteomics, qPCR, DNA sequencing, flow cytometry, particle analysis, X-ray diffraction, thermal analysis, electrochemistry, birefringence imaging and protein stability analysis. See &lt;a href="https://www.scichart.com/why-scichart-diagnostics-lifesciences/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;SciChart for diagnostics and life sciences&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Can SciChart be used for proteomics and protein analysis?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. SciChart supports peptide plots, spectra, overlays and compound comparisons, and is used for protein stability analysis. NanoTemper uses SciChart to present protein stability data from its Prometheus instruments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Can SciChart be used for X-ray diffraction, thermal analysis and electrochemistry?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. SciChart plots diffraction profiles, temperature sweeps, time-resolved data, voltage and charge-discharge curves and impedance plots, with multiple axes and overlays.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Can SciChart be used for DNA sequencing and birefringence imaging?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. SciChart lists DNA sequencing and birefringence imaging among its diagnostics and life sciences applications, alongside mass spectrometry, gas chromatography, qPCR and spectroscopy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Can SciChart visualize spectral frequency analysis?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. SciChart displays frequency spectra, spectrograms and heatmaps, so teams can analyze spectral frequencies in real time alongside the raw signal.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Can SciChart zoom and pan through large scientific datasets?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. Zooming and panning, peak isolation, cursors, logarithmic axes in scientific notation and multiple axes work on large datasets out of the box.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Does SciChart support remote viewing of instrument data?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. Teams typically use SciChart WPF in the desktop application at the instrument and SciChart.js for remote viewing in the browser, with consistent chart behavior in both.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Is SciChart a 64-bit charting library?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. SciChart is a true 64-bit library: its charts, series and API functions can use double-precision floating point, with scientific notation and logarithmic scales for demanding analytical work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Can SciChart.js handle billion-point scientific datasets in the browser?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. Its optional 64-bit WebAssembly mode gives charts up to 16 GB of browser memory, enough for around 1 billion data points, so full-resolution scientific data can be reviewed without server-side downsampling. It runs in Chrome, Edge and Firefox; other browsers use the standard engine, which holds around 250 million points.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


What are the best charting components for scientific applications?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The best charting components for scientific applications combine GPU rendering, scientific chart types, rich annotation and a consistent API across platforms. SciChart offers these for JavaScript, React, WPF, iOS and Android; Plotly, Chart.js, VTK and D3.js each cover narrower use cases.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Can SciChart show raw data without resampling?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. Resampling is on by default and designed to be visually lossless, but you can switch it off per series to present data exactly as acquired, with some performance cost on very large datasets.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Can SciChart charts match my company's branding?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. Themes, colors, fonts, dark and light modes and user-configurable palettes let you white-label the charts across your suite.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Does SciChart support 3D scientific visualization?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. SciChart offers 3D charts such as surfaces, waterfalls and scatter plots, built on the same GPU-accelerated engine as its 2D charts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


SciChart vs Plotly: which is better for scientific applications?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Plotly suits exploratory analysis in Python, R and the browser. SciChart suits shipped instrument software that needs GPU-rendered charts across JavaScript, WPF and mobile, with large datasets and more charts per screen than Plotly's documented 4 to 8 WebGL figures per page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


What is the best WPF chart control for scientific instruments?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SciChart WPF is built for scientific instrument software, rendering billions of points per dataset on a DirectX engine, with an API consistent with SciChart.js for browser-based review tools.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


What is the best JavaScript chart library for scientific data?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For large or real-time scientific datasets, a WebGL and WebAssembly library such as SciChart.js scales furthest; it can render up to 1 billion points in browsers that support 64-bit WebAssembly. For small datasets and standard charts, Chart.js is simpler.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Is there a free version of SciChart?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. The SciChart.js community license is free for non-commercial use and commercial evaluation. It carries a watermark, which a paid license removes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Is there a SciChart Python library?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No. SciChart does not offer a Python library. Teams working in Python notebooks often use Plotly or Matplotlib for analysis and SciChart in the shipped instrument application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


What are the best data visualization libraries for scientific applications?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For shipped scientific applications with large or real-time datasets, GPU-accelerated charting libraries such as SciChart lead on performance and analysis tooling. Plotly suits exploratory analysis, VTK suits 3D volumes, Chart.js suits small web datasets, and D3.js suits bespoke graphics.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Which scientific visualization tools do research labs use?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Research labs typically combine analysis tools such as Python with Plotly or Matplotlib, instrument software from vendors like Waters, Thermo Fisher and Agilent, and custom applications built on charting libraries such as SciChart for real-time and large-dataset work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How do I draw a mass spectrum as a stick plot with m/z labels on the major peaks, without labels overlapping?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use an impulse (stem) series with data labels. SciChart.js &lt;a href="https://www.scichart.com/documentation/js/v5/2d-charts/chart-types/data-point-labels/data-label-skip-modes-and-culling/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;data labels can hide overlapping labels&lt;/a&gt;, skip points, or appear only when there is room, and a custom label layout manager can resolve overlaps across several overlaid spectra.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How do I link a TIC chromatogram to the mass spectrum at a clicked retention time?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use the hit-test API on the chromatogram series to find the data point under the click, then load that scan into the spectrum chart's data series. Linked charts keep zoom and cursors in step, so analysts can move between the TIC, XICs and spectra without losing their place.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How do I plot NMR or FTIR spectra with a reversed x-axis (ppm or wavenumber decreasing left to right)?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Set &lt;code&gt;flippedCoordinates&lt;/code&gt; on the X axis. Zoom, pan, annotations and hit-testing all follow the reversed axis, so you do not need to transform the data.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How do I display diode array data (time × wavelength × absorbance) as a heatmap with contour lines?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Plot the matrix as a uniform heatmap with a color map, then overlay a &lt;a href="https://www.scichart.com/documentation/js/v4/2d-charts/chart-types/uniform-contours-renderable-series/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;contours series&lt;/a&gt; on the same data. Use the non-uniform heatmap when wavelength or time steps are uneven.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How do I let analysts drag integration limits or mark a peak region by hand?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use &lt;a href="https://scichart.com/example/javascript-chart/editable-annotations" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;editable annotations&lt;/a&gt;: vertical line or box annotations with &lt;code&gt;isEditable&lt;/code&gt; set can be dragged on the chart at data coordinates. Read their X positions to define the integration window; the integration itself stays in your application code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How do I keep zoom and cursors synchronized across stacked detector traces, such as UV, MS and pressure?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Link the charts so they share the X range and cursor. SciChart.js has a dedicated tutorial on linking multiple charts, and SciChart WPF supports the same pattern for stacked panels.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How do I stream detector data for hours without memory growth?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use a data series in FIFO mode with a fixed capacity, so old points drop off as new ones arrive, and append in batches rather than point by point. Declaring that X is sorted and contains no NaN values lets SciChart skip those checks on every append.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How do I show a gap where acquisition paused or a detector dropped out?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Append a NaN value at the gap so the line breaks instead of joining across missing data.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How do I keep full precision for high-resolution m/z values or nanosecond timestamps?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SciChart is a 64-bit library: data series, axes and API functions use double-precision floating point, so values with many significant figures or large offsets are not rounded the way 32-bit GPU pipelines can round them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How do I render a 96- or 384-well plate of qPCR amplification curves on one screen?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use the &lt;a href="https://scichart.com/documentation/js/current/SubChartsWorkedExample10x10Grid.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;SubCharts API&lt;/a&gt;, which places a grid of small charts on one surface sharing a single WebGL context. SciChart's worked example builds a 10×10 grid, and the same approach scales to plate layouts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Can I reuse chart code between a WPF acquisition app and a SciChart.js review tool?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not directly: one is C# and the other is TypeScript. The concepts (series, axes, modifiers, annotations, themes) mirror each other, so you can match behavior and styling across both, and SciChart.js charts can be serialized to JSON to save and restore chart state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How do I qualify SciChart as SOUP under IEC 62304?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Pin a specific version, record it in your SOUP list, review the published changelog and known issues against your device's intended use, and monitor new releases for anomalies. SciChart's versioned releases and direct support make that evidence easier to gather; the qualification itself remains your responsibility.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Can SciChart be used in regulated diagnostic or medical software?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SciChart is used in rigorously assessed environments, including cancer diagnostics research with the Mayo Clinic. As a commercial component with versioned releases, documented limitations and direct support, it is easier to qualify than an unsupported library.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;React, deeply&lt;/strong&gt; · Chapter 3 of 12 · Web · new chapter every Monday night&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;By the end of this chapter:&lt;/strong&gt; Identify the small number of cases useEffect is for, and the far larger number where it is the wrong tool.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Emma, a front-end engineer, is working on a real-time analytics dashboard with a two-month sprint deadline. The dashboard updates a table every second with live metrics from a WebSocket. After adding a "filter by region" feature, the dashboard started experiencing issues: the table lags by up to three seconds, the CPU usage on the user's laptop spikes to 90%, and the performance monitoring alert fires every time a user opens the page. The team cannot afford to roll back the feature, and the sprint deadline is only three days away. Emma needs to identify the cause of the problem and fix it quickly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The issue seems to be related to the way the state is being updated and the effects that are being run after each update. Emma has been using &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; to handle the side effects of updating the table, but she's not sure if she's using it correctly. She's also not sure if there are other factors contributing to the performance issues.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Emma's current implementation uses &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; to fetch new data from the WebSocket and update the table every second. However, she's noticed that the &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; hook is being run more frequently than expected, causing the CPU usage to spike. She's tried to optimize the code by using &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt;, but the issue persists.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Before you start
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To follow along with this chapter, you should have a good understanding of React's rendering and reconciliation process, as well as the different state mechanisms available in React, such as &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt;. You should also have a basic understanding of how to use &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; to handle side effects in your React components.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Make sure you have the following installed:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Node.js (version 16 or later)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;npm (version 8 or later)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;React (version 18 or later)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A code editor or IDE of your choice&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can verify that you have the correct versions installed by running the following commands in your terminal:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;node -v
npm -v
npm ls react
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Understanding useEffect
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; is a powerful hook in React that allows you to run side effects after rendering a component. However, it's often misused or overused, leading to performance issues like the one Emma is experiencing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To understand how &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; works, let's break down its signature:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;effect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dependencies&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;effect&lt;/code&gt; function is the code that will be run after rendering the component, and the &lt;code&gt;dependencies&lt;/code&gt; array specifies which values the effect depends on. If any of the values in the &lt;code&gt;dependencies&lt;/code&gt; array change, the effect will be re-run.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


When to use useEffect
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So, when should you use &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;? Here are some scenarios where &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; is the right tool for the job:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Fetching data from an API or WebSocket&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Setting up event listeners or subscriptions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Updating the DOM directly (although this should be avoided whenever possible)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, there are many cases where &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; is not the right tool. For example:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Updating state based on user input&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Running computations that don't have side effects&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Handling errors or exceptions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In these cases, other hooks like &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt;, or &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt; might be more suitable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Build it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's take a look at Emma's code and see where she might be going wrong. Here's an example of her &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; hook:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'react'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Dashboard&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setData&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setFilter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'all'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;socket&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;WebSocket&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'ws://example.com/data'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;socket&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;onmessage&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setData&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;parse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;socket&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;close&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;table&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;tr&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;td&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;td&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;td&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;td&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;tr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;table&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The problem with this code is that the &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; hook is being run every time the &lt;code&gt;filter&lt;/code&gt; state changes, which is causing the WebSocket connection to be reopened and closed repeatedly. This is likely causing the CPU usage to spike and the table to lag.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To fix this issue, Emma can use the &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; hook with an empty &lt;code&gt;dependencies&lt;/code&gt; array, like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;socket&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;WebSocket&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'ws://example.com/data'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;socket&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;onmessage&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setData&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;parse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;socket&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;close&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This will ensure that the &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; hook is only run once, when the component mounts, and not every time the &lt;code&gt;filter&lt;/code&gt; state changes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


When this breaks
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are several ways that the &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; hook can break, including:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Running the effect too frequently, causing performance issues&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Failing to clean up after the effect, causing memory leaks&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Not handling errors or exceptions properly&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To avoid these issues, make sure to:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use the &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; hook with caution and only when necessary&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Always clean up after the effect by returning a function from the &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; hook&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Handle errors and exceptions properly using try-catch blocks and error boundaries&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What it costs
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Using &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; can come with a cost, including:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Performance issues if the effect is run too frequently&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Memory leaks if the effect is not cleaned up properly&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Complexity and debugging issues if the effect is not handled properly&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To minimize these costs, make sure to:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use the &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; hook only when necessary&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Optimize the effect to run as infrequently as possible&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use tools like the React DevTools to debug and optimize the effect&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


In the interview
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When interviewing for a front-end engineering position, you may be asked questions about &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; and how to use it properly. Here are some example questions and answers:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Q: What is &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; and how does it work?&lt;br&gt;
A: &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; is a hook in React that allows you to run side effects after rendering a component. It takes two arguments: the effect function and the dependencies array. The effect function is run after rendering the component, and the dependencies array specifies which values the effect depends on.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Q: When should you use &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;?&lt;br&gt;
A: You should use &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; when you need to run a side effect after rendering a component, such as fetching data from an API or setting up event listeners.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Q: What are some common mistakes people make when using &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;?&lt;br&gt;
A: Some common mistakes people make when using &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; include running the effect too frequently, failing to clean up after the effect, and not handling errors or exceptions properly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Your tasks
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Create a simple React component that uses &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; to fetch data from an API and update the component's state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Optimize the component to run the effect as infrequently as possible.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use the React DevTools to debug and optimize the component.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Write a test for the component to ensure it is working correctly.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Refactor the component to use a different hook, such as &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt;, to see if it improves performance.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;




